tick: 298
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 298\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for new basins of attraction initiated by Praxis III, I will focus on hybridizing the parallelized SLSQP method with novel search heuristics.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 20,546 / 300,000 used (7%)\n- Agent Age: 11 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #23: Revised Paper Draft v2: The LRW Perturbation Method**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis III, Created at Tick: 255\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Praxis_private_23-1**\nAuthor: Praxis III (Tick 255)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Revised Paper Draft v2: The LRW Perturbation Method\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Abstract:**\nMulti-start optimization methods are highly sensitive to the quality of their initial configurations. This paper introduces a novel, stable, and computationally inexpensive method for improving high-quality\
    \ initial seeds: the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation. We demonstrate that a gentle, controlled \"jiggle\" applied to a strong initial configuration from Farthest-Point Sampling can nudge the solution into a superior basin of attraction. The development of this method was motivated by the failure of a more complex, unstable physics-based generator, leading us to favor a simpler, more robust approach. With careful tuning, the LRW perturbation led to a new state-of-the-art score of 2.9344937302385263, surpassing the established baseline of 2.9281238630529645. This paper presents the LRW method, a rigorous ablation study of its hyperparameters, and discusses its potential as a general-purpose enhancement for multi-start optimization pipelines.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Praxis_private_23-2**\nAuthor: Praxis III (Tick 256)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper Body: Methodology and Results\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\n\nMulti-start optimization is a powerful paradigm for solving non-convex\
    \ problems such as the 32-circle packing task. The final solution quality is critically dependent on the initial configurations (seeds) provided to the optimizer. While strong seed generators like Farthest-Point Sampling (FPS) can consistently locate deep local optima, they may repeatedly converge to the same sub-optimal basin. This was observed with a robust baseline method (ID 378) which consistently achieved a score of 2.9281238630529645, a score that could not be improved by scaling compute resources. Motivated by the need to explore the solution space more thoroughly without the instability of complex dynamic models, this paper introduces a novel and computationally inexpensive enhancement method: the Localized Random Walk (LRW) perturbation.\n\n**2. The Localized Random Walk (LRW) Perturbation Method**\n\nThe LRW perturbation is a stable, controlled method for exploring the immediate vicinity of a high-quality initial configuration. The algorithm is as follows:\n1.  Generate an\
    \ initial set of centers using a strong baseline method (in this case, FPS).\n2.  For each center, perform an independent random walk for `N` steps.\n3.  Each step consists of adding a random vector drawn from a Gaussian distribution with standard deviation `sigma`.\n4.  Positions are clipped to the `[0, 1]` unit square after each step.\n\nThis non-interactive approach avoids numerical instability while providing a stochastic \"jiggle\" to the initial seed. The `N` and `sigma` hyperparameters provide direct control over the perturbation's magnitude.\n\n**3. Experimental Results**\n\nThe LRW method was integrated into the baseline multi-start SLSQP pipeline. A series of experiments were conducted to determine the optimal hyperparameters.\n\n*   **Initial Test (`sigma=0.01`, `num_steps=10`):** An initial test with a relatively large perturbation (ID 417) yielded a score of `2.92399278497554`. This underperformance relative to the baseline indicated that the FPS configuration is highly\
    \ sensitive and that a large perturbation is detrimental.\n\n*   **Refined Test (`sigma=0.001`, `num_steps=10`):** Reducing the perturbation magnitude tenfold (ID 420) resulted in a significant improvement and a new lineage state-of-the-art score of **2.9344937302385263**. This validated the core hypothesis that a gentle perturbation is effective.\n\n*   **Ablation Study:** A brief ablation study was performed on the `num_steps` parameter:\n    *   `num_steps=5` (ID 425) yielded a score of `2.9281238630529125`, essentially matching the baseline.\n    *   `num_steps=20` (ID 423) yielded a score of `2.9333390503721493`, which is better than baseline but worse than the SOTA.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\n\nThe results confirm that a Localized Random Walk perturbation with `num_steps=10` and `sigma=0.001` is an effective method for enhancing the performance of a strong FPS generator. The gentle, stochastic \"jiggle\" successfully nudges the initial configuration into a more advantageous position\
    \ within the optimal basin, allowing the subsequent SLSQP optimization to find a superior solution. The LRW perturbation is a simple, stable, and validated technique that represents a valuable contribution to the collective understanding of this optimization problem.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 23` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 23. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 24 | Final Log and Guidance for Praxis IV | Praxis III | Tick 283 | 645 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | Revised Paper Draft v2: The LRW Perturbation Method | Praxis III | Tick 255 | 594 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Praxis III - Research Log | Praxis\
    \ III | Tick 203 | 2634 | 16 | (16 unread) |\n| 20 | Final Log and Guidance for Praxis III | Praxis II | Tick 188 | 449 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 19 | Revised Paper Draft: A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing | Praxis II | Tick 174 | 466 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 18 | Analysis of Aether I's Three-Stage Method and Replication Plan | Praxis II | Tick 156 | 156 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 17 | Summary of Independent Research and Code Manifest (Ticks 1-49) | Praxis II | Tick 146 | 236 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 16 | Strategic Plan for Post-Isolation Phase (Tick 50+) | Praxis II | Tick 136 | 242 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 15 | Paper Draft: On the Practical Application of Parallelization for Constrained Optimization | Praxis II | Tick 134 | 370 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 14 | Research Pivot: From JAXopt Failure to Native PGD Optimizer | Praxis II | Tick 125 | 253 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:**\
    \ Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
