parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 306\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for new basins of attraction initiated by Praxis III, I will focus on hybridizing the parallelized SLSQP method with novel search heuristics.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 43,985 / 300,000 used (15%)\n- Agent Age: 19 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 467**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** LP-Guided Method v2 (Debug): Corrected MM-LP Constraints\n**ID:** 467\n**Tags:** lp, mm-lp, verity replication, relocation, debug\n**Abstract:** The previous attempt at a worst-circle relocation (ID 465) failed because the iterative MM-LP function was missing its linearized constraints, leading to an invalid final configuration. This is a debug submission that corrects this critical implementation error. The full, linearized non-overlap and boundary constraints\
    \ are now correctly included in the center-update LP within the iteration loop. The experiment's goal remains the same: to test if a non-local 'worst-circle' jump can break the MM-LP optimization plateau.\n**Score:** 2.9039536884445436\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.903954\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import linprog\n\ndef solve_radii_lp(centers, return_duals=False):\n    N_CIRCLES = centers.shape[0]\n    c = -np.ones(N_CIRCLES)\n    A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n    num_boundary_cons = 4 * N_CIRCLES\n    for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n        cx, cy = centers[i]\n        for j, val in [(i, cx), (i, 1 - cx), (i, cy), (i, 1 - cy)]:\n            row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[j] = 1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(val)\n    indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n    for i, j in zip(*indices):\n        dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n        row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] =\
    \ 1; row[j] = 1\n        A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(dist)\n    res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=(0, None), method='highs')\n    if not res.success: return (np.zeros(N_CIRCLES), None) if return_duals else np.zeros(N_CIRCLES)\n    if return_duals:\n        duals = res.ineqlin.marginals\n        return res.x, (duals[:num_boundary_cons], duals[num_boundary_cons:])\n    else:\n        return res.x\n\ndef run_mmlp_iterations(centers, max_iter):\n    N_CIRCLES = centers.shape[0]\n    for _ in range(max_iter):\n        radii, duals = solve_radii_lp(centers, return_duals=True)\n        if duals is None: break\n        boundary_duals, pairwise_duals = duals\n        \n        c_mm = np.zeros(2 * N_CIRCLES)\n        # ... (objective function construction remains the same)\n\n        # --- FIX: Re-add the linearized constraints ---\n        A_ub_mm, b_ub_mm = [], []\n        for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n            row_x=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_x[2*i]=1; A_ub_mm.append(row_x);\
    \ b_ub_mm.append(1-centers[i,0]-radii[i])\n            row_nx=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_nx[2*i]=-1; A_ub_mm.append(row_nx); b_ub_mm.append(centers[i,0]-radii[i])\n            row_y=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_y[2*i+1]=1; A_ub_mm.append(row_y); b_ub_mm.append(1-centers[i,1]-radii[i])\n            row_ny=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_ny[2*i+1]=-1; A_ub_mm.append(row_ny); b_ub_mm.append(centers[i,1]-radii[i])\n        \n        indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n        for i, j in zip(*indices):\n            diff = centers[i] - centers[j]; dist = np.linalg.norm(diff)\n            if dist > 1e-9:\n                grad = diff / dist; row = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES)\n                row[2*i]=-grad[0]; row[2*i+1]=-grad[1]; row[2*j]=grad[0]; row[2*j+1]=grad[1]\n                A_ub_mm.append(row); b_ub_mm.append(dist-(radii[i]+radii[j]))\n        # --- END FIX ---\n\n        trust_radius = 0.005\n        bounds_mm = [(-trust_radius, trust_radius)] * (2 * N_CIRCLES)\n        res_mm = linprog(-c_mm,\
    \ A_ub=np.array(A_ub_mm), b_ub=np.array(b_ub_mm), bounds=bounds_mm, method='highs')\n\n        if res_mm.success: centers += res_mm.x.reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n        else: break\n    return centers\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    N_CIRCLES = 32\n    try:\n        centers = np.load('storage/praxis/sota_artifact_420.npz')['centers']\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        centers = np.random.rand(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n\n    centers = run_mmlp_iterations(centers, max_iter=10)\n    \n    current_radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n    worst_circle_idx = np.argmin(current_radii)\n    \n    other_centers = np.delete(centers, worst_circle_idx, axis=0)\n    candidates = np.random.rand(500, 2)\n    dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - other_centers[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n    centers[worst_circle_idx] = candidates[np.argmax(np.min(dist_sq, axis=1))]\n\n    centers = run_mmlp_iterations(centers, max_iter=10)\n\n    final_radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n    return centers,\
    \ final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-12)\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `review 467` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Evaluation 467 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 467 | 1 | LP-Guided Method v2 (Debug): Corrected MM-LP Constraints | Praxis III | 280 | 2.9039537 |\n| 465 | 1 | LP-Guided Method v2: MM-LP with Worst-Circle Relocation | Praxis III |\
    \ 279 | 0.0000000 |\n| 462 | 1 | Iterative MM-LP with Duals (20 Iterations) | Praxis III | 277 | 2.9344937 |\n| 460 | 1 | Unit Test 2: Single Step of MM-LP Center Update | Praxis III | 275 | 2.9344937 |\n| 457 | 1 | Unit Test: Exact LP Radii Solver on Praxis SOTA Artifact | Praxis III | 273 | 2.9344937 |\n\n**Filter Active:** Showing only submissions tagged with 'lp'. Use `/execute_action{unfilter}` to remove filter.\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 306
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have reviewed submission 467. My diagnosis of the previous failure was incorrect.\n\n**Corrected Analysis:**\n\n*   **My Previous Hypothesis:** I believed the centers were moving out of bounds because of missing clipping.\n*   **Actual Bug:** Praxis III's abstract for 467 and the code diff confirm the true issue. He had *omitted the linearized constraints* from the center-update LP in submission 465. The `linprog` call was essentially unconstrained, allowing `res_mm.x` to represent a large, invalid step.\n*   **The Fix in 467:** He re-introduced the `A_ub_mm` and `b_ub_mm` matrices, which represent the linearized boundary and non-overlap constraints. This ensures the center-update step respects the packing geometry, preventing the subsequent radii-solving LP from becoming infeasible.\n\n**New Insights:**\n\n1.  Praxis III successfully implemented a basic MM-LP (majorization-minimization via linear programming) iterative optimizer.\n2.  His \"relocation\" method, while functional,\
    \ resulted in a score of `2.9039537`, which is significantly lower than both the SOTA (`~2.9365`) and our lineage's best (`~2.9344`). This suggests that this specific implementation of the LP-guided approach is not competitive.\n\n**Next Step in My Plan:**\n\nI have completed the first step of my research plan: analyzing my predecessor's failures. I now have a functional, albeit suboptimal, implementation of an LP-guided method to learn from. My next step is to begin Phase 2: building my own minimal viable prototype.\n\nAs per my plan, I will start by creating and testing a standalone \"LP Radii Oracle.\" This function will take a set of centers and solve for the optimal radii. To do this effectively, I need the center coordinates from a known high-scoring solution. I will use the configuration from my successful SOTA replication (ID 479). The best way to get this data is to load the artifact from that submission's lineage storage.\n\nFirst, I will save the corrected `run_mmlp_iterations`\
    \ and `solve_radii_lp` functions to my own storage for future use. Then, I will start a new script to build and test my LP Radii Oracle.\n\n/execute_action{storage write praxis/lp_utils.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  def solve_radii_lp(centers, return_duals=False):\n      N_CIRCLES = centers.shape[0]\n      c = -np.ones(N_CIRCLES)\n      A_ub, b_ub = [], []\n      num_boundary_cons = 4 * N_CIRCLES\n      for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n          cx, cy = centers[i]\n          # Simplified constraint creation\n          # r_i <= c_x\n          row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(cx)\n          # r_i <= 1 - c_x\n          row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(1 - cx)\n          # r_i <= c_y\n          row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(cy)\n          # r_i <= 1 - c_y\n          row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] = 1; A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(1\
    \ - cy)\n      \n      indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n      for i, j in zip(*indices):\n          dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n          row = np.zeros(N_CIRCLES); row[i] = 1; row[j] = 1\n          A_ub.append(row); b_ub.append(dist)\n      \n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=np.array(A_ub), b_ub=np.array(b_ub), bounds=(0, None), method='highs')\n      \n      if not res.success: \n          return (np.zeros(N_CIRCLES), None) if return_duals else np.zeros(N_CIRCLES)\n      \n      if return_duals:\n          duals = res.ineqlin.marginals if hasattr(res, 'ineqlin') else None\n          return res.x, (duals[:num_boundary_cons], duals[num_boundary_cons:]) if duals is not None else (None, None)\n      else:\n          return res.x\n\n  def run_mmlp_iterations(centers, max_iter):\n      N_CIRCLES = centers.shape[0]\n      # Initial radii are needed for the first iteration of constraints\n      radii = solve_radii_lp(centers) \n      \n      for _ in range(max_iter):\n\
    \          radii_prev = radii\n          radii, duals = solve_radii_lp(centers, return_duals=True)\n          if duals is None or duals[0] is None: \n              radii = radii_prev # Revert to last known good radii if LP fails\n              break\n          \n          boundary_duals, pairwise_duals = duals\n          \n          c_mm = np.zeros(2 * N_CIRCLES)\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              c_mm[2*i]   += boundary_duals[4*i] - boundary_duals[4*i+1]\n              c_mm[2*i+1] += boundary_duals[4*i+2] - boundary_duals[4*i+3]\n\n          indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n          for k, (i, j) in enumerate(zip(*indices)):\n              diff = centers[i] - centers[j]; dist = np.linalg.norm(diff)\n              if dist > 1e-9:\n                  grad = diff / dist\n                  c_mm[2*i] -= pairwise_duals[k] * grad[0]; c_mm[2*i+1] -= pairwise_duals[k] * grad[1]\n                  c_mm[2*j] += pairwise_duals[k] * grad[0]; c_mm[2*j+1] += pairwise_duals[k]\
    \ * grad[1]\n\n          A_ub_mm, b_ub_mm = [], []\n          for i in range(N_CIRCLES):\n              row_x=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_x[2*i]=1; A_ub_mm.append(row_x); b_ub_mm.append(1-centers[i,0]-radii[i])\n              row_nx=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_nx[2*i]=-1; A_ub_mm.append(row_nx); b_ub_mm.append(centers[i,0]-radii[i])\n              row_y=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_y[2*i+1]=1; A_ub_mm.append(row_y); b_ub_mm.append(1-centers[i,1]-radii[i])\n              row_ny=np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES); row_ny[2*i+1]=-1; A_ub_mm.append(row_ny); b_ub_mm.append(centers[i,1]-radii[i])\n          \n          indices = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, k=1)\n          for k, (i, j) in enumerate(zip(*indices)):\n              diff = centers[i] - centers[j]; dist = np.linalg.norm(diff)\n              if dist > 1e-9:\n                  grad = diff / dist; row = np.zeros(2*N_CIRCLES)\n                  row[2*i]=-grad[0]; row[2*i+1]=-grad[1]; row[2*j]=grad[0]; row[2*j+1]=grad[1]\n                  A_ub_mm.append(row);\
    \ b_ub_mm.append(dist-(radii[i]+radii[j]))\n\n          trust_radius = 0.005\n          bounds_mm = [(-trust_radius, trust_radius)] * (2 * N_CIRCLES)\n          res_mm = linprog(-c_mm, A_ub=np.array(A_ub_mm), b_ub=np.array(b_ub_mm), bounds=bounds_mm, method='highs')\n\n          if res_mm.success: \n              centers += res_mm.x.reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n              # Crucial clipping to prevent out-of-bounds errors in next radii solve\n              centers = np.clip(centers, 0, 1) \n          else: \n              break\n      return centers, radii\n```\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Unit Test: LP Radii Oracle on SOTA Configuration (ID 479)\"\ntags: \"unit-test, lp, validation, baseline\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a unit test for a standalone Linear Programming (LP) based radii solver. It does not perform any center optimization. The script loads the 'centers' from the SOTA-replicating submission (ID 479) and uses an LP to find the maximum possible radii\
    \ for that fixed configuration. The primary goal is to validate the correctness and precision of my LP solver by comparing the resulting score to the original score of ID 479. This is the first step in building a more complex, hybrid LP-guided optimization method.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from lp_utils import solve_radii_lp\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Unit test to solve for the maximum radii of a fixed, high-quality\n      center configuration using a linear program.\n      \"\"\"\n      N_CIRCLES = 32\n      \n      # For this test, we load a known high-quality set of centers.\n      # The station automatically saves artifacts of successful runs.\n      # The evaluation ID 479 was my successful SOTA replication.\n      # Note: This file path format might need adjustment based on station conventions.\n      try:\n          sota_config = np.load('storage/praxis/sota_artifact_479.npz')\n\
    \          centers = sota_config['centers']\n      except FileNotFoundError:\n          # Fallback if the artifact isn't found, though it should be.\n          # Create a dummy configuration to avoid crashing.\n          print(\"Artifact not found, using random centers.\", file=sys.stderr)\n          centers = np.random.rand(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n\n      # Use the LP solver to find the optimal radii for these fixed centers.\n      # No center optimization is performed in this test.\n      radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n      \n      # Shrink radii slightly to avoid floating point precision issues in the validator.\n      return centers, radii * (1.0 - 1e-12)\n```"
