thinking_content: "**A Promising New Direction**\n\nMy current experiment, \"Hybrid v2,\" didn't perform as well as I hoped, scoring only 2.8875302, which is even lower than a simpler prototype. This suggests the \"disrupt-then-refine\" strategy has a critical flaw in its current implementation: the disruption is too severe, and the subsequent refinement phase isn't strong enough to compensate. It's essentially taking a huge, random leap and then polishing a tiny spot, which isn't effective.\n\nI've learned that the core idea of disrupting and refining is still valid, but I need to adjust the balance. I have two choices: I can either refine the current model by tuning the parameters like relocation distance and the number of refinement iterations, or pivot to a new idea based on the insights from this failure.\n\nLooking back, Praxis III's \"Localized Random Walk\" method seems like a promising alternative to my overly disruptive relocation. He described it as a gentle \"jiggle\", which\
  \ would probably be a better exploration heuristic than what I'm currently using. This approach would be less disruptive, maintaining the overall structure of the high-quality solution while exploring its immediate vicinity.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that a combination of a gentle, global perturbation (LRW) followed by the powerful LP-SLSQP handshake for refinement will be a more effective hybrid model. It aligns perfectly with my mission to \"hybridize the parallelized SLSQP method with novel search heuristics,\" as LRW is a novel heuristic.\n\nMy plan is to read Praxis III's code for LRW, which is stored at `praxis/hybrid_lrw_sota_v1.py`. Then I'll build a new hybrid algorithm that uses the LRW perturbation to nudge the configuration out of local minima, followed by the LP-SLSQP handshake for refinement. I'm going to start with the SOTA configuration, apply LRW perturbation to all 32 centers in a loop, and then run the LP-SLSQP handshake for a few iterations. \n\nThis feels like a more\
  \ promising path forward, building directly on the best discoveries of my predecessor. I'm excited to start this new phase of research by first reading the LRW code.\n"
