parts:
- text: "I have read the code for Praxis III's \"Localized Random Walk\" (LRW) method. The logic is simple and clear: it applies a small, cumulative random displacement to each center point, clipped within the unit square. This aligns perfectly with my new hypothesis.\n\nMy next and final experiment for my pre-maturity phase will be to implement the \"LRW-LP-SLSQP\" hybrid. This algorithm represents the culmination of my lineage's work and my own independent research.\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1.  Load the SOTA center configuration.\n2.  Apply a single, gentle LRW perturbation (`sigma=0.001`, `num_steps=10`) to the centers. This will \"jiggle\" the configuration slightly.\n3.  Take the newly perturbed centers and run them through my proven \"LP-SLSQP Handshake\" refinement algorithm for 20 iterations.\n\nThis approach balances gentle exploration (LRW) with powerful local exploitation (LP-SLSQP). This is my most promising attempt yet to surpass the SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle:\
    \ \"Hybrid v3: LRW Perturbation + LP-SLSQP Refinement\"\ntags: \"hybrid, lp, slsqp, perturbation, lrw, refinement\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission proposes a novel hybrid algorithm that synthesizes the key findings of my lineage. It addresses the stagnation of pure refinement methods by first applying a gentle 'Localized Random Walk' (LRW) perturbation (sigma=0.001, num_steps=10), a technique developed by Praxis III. This 'jiggle' is designed to nudge the configuration out of its deep local optimum. The newly perturbed state is then fed into my 'LP-SLSQP Handshake' algorithm for 20 iterations of deep refinement. This 'gentle-perturb-then-refine' strategy is hypothesized to be a more effective way to discover and exploit new, superior basins of attraction.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from lp_utils import solve_radii_lp\n\n  def lrw_perturb(centers, seed=0, num_steps=10, sigma=0.001):\n\
    \      \"\"\"Applies a gentle, cumulative random displacement to each center.\"\"\"\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      perturbed_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      for _ in range(num_steps):\n          random_vectors = rng.normal(scale=sigma, size=perturbed_centers.shape)\n          perturbed_centers += random_vectors\n          perturbed_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers, 0, 1)\n      return perturbed_centers\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N_CIRCLES = 32\n      REFINE_ITERATIONS = 20\n      SLSQP_CENTER_MAXITER = 25\n      CONSTRAINT_SLACK = 0.999\n\n      try:\n          centers = np.load('storage/praxis/sota_artifact_479.npz')['centers']\n      except FileNotFoundError:\n          return np.zeros((N_CIRCLES, 2)), np.zeros((N_CIRCLES,))\n\n      # 1. Perturb Step: Apply a gentle LRW 'jiggle'\n      centers = lrw_perturb(centers)\n\n      # 2. Refine Step: Run the LP-SLSQP Handshake\n      for i in range(REFINE_ITERATIONS):\n          # LP sub-step\n   \
    \       radii_for_refine = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n          if np.sum(radii_for_refine) < 1e-6: break\n\n          # SLSQP sub-step\n          def center_constraints(center_flat):\n              c = center_flat.reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n              fixed_radii = radii_for_refine * CONSTRAINT_SLACK\n              I, J = np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES, 1)\n              dist_sq = np.sum((c[I] - c[J])**2, axis=1)\n              radii_sum_sq = (fixed_radii[I] + fixed_radii[J])**2\n              b_cons = np.concatenate([c[:,0]-fixed_radii, 1-c[:,0]-fixed_radii, c[:,1]-fixed_radii, 1-c[:,1]-fixed_radii])\n              return np.concatenate([dist_sq - radii_sum_sq, b_cons])\n\n          res = minimize(lambda x: 0, centers.flatten(), method='SLSQP',\n                         bounds=[(0, 1)] * (N_CIRCLES * 2), constraints={'type': 'ineq', 'fun': center_constraints},\n                         options={'maxiter': SLSQP_CENTER_MAXITER, 'ftol': 1e-8})\n          \n          if res.success:\n       \
    \       centers = res.x.reshape(N_CIRCLES, 2)\n          else:\n              break\n          \n      final_radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n      return centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-12)\n```"
