tick: 322
role: model
parts:
- text: "My debugged Hybrid Adaptive Search (ID 526) ran successfully, but the result is a significant finding in itself.\n\n**Analysis of Result (ID 526):**\n\n1.  **Success:** The seeding fix was correct, and the algorithm produced a valid, high-quality result without errors.\n2.  **Underperformance:** The score `2.9321688` is substantially *lower* than the SOTA (`~2.9365`).\n3.  **Critical Insight:** My hypothesis was that my \"superior\" refinement engine would produce a better result. The data contradicts this. This implies one of two things:\n    a) The standard, integrated SLSQP refiner is simply better at this task than my decoupled LP-SLSQP Handshake.\n    b) My LP-SLSQP Handshake is too computationally expensive. The 20-iteration refinement loop for each of the 32 elite candidates might be causing the overall process to hit the 20-minute timeout before all candidates can be fully explored, leading to a suboptimal final selection.\n\nHypothesis (b) is the most likely and testable.\
    \ A more complex refinement stage naturally takes longer. It is plausible that the original SOTA method is faster, allowing it to complete its search more thoroughly within the time limit.\n\n**Next Experiment: Testing the Computational Cost Hypothesis**\n\nTo test this, I will reduce the complexity of my refinement stage. I will cut the number of `REFINE_HANDSHAKE_ITERATIONS` in half, from 20 to 10. If my theory is correct, this will allow each of the 32 parallel refinement workers to finish faster, ensuring the entire elite set is processed before any potential timeout. While each individual refinement will be less deep, a more complete search across the entire candidate pool may yield a better overall result.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid Adaptive Search v1.2: Reduced Refinement Complexity\"\ntags: \"hybrid, adaptive-search, lp, slsqp, refinement, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment investigates the hypothesis that the previous version (ID 526)\
    \ underperformed due to high computational cost leading to a timeout. This submission is identical to v1.1 but reduces the refinement complexity by cutting the number of 'LP-SLSQP Handshake' iterations from 20 to 10 per candidate. The goal is to test if a shallower but more complete search across all 32 elite candidates can produce a better score within the station's time constraints.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  from multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from lp_utils import solve_radii_lp\n\n  N_CIRCLES = 32\n  N_PROSPECT = 1024\n  MAXITER_PROSPECT = 60\n  N_ELITE = 32\n  # --- CHANGE: Reduced refinement iterations to test computational cost hypothesis ---\n  REFINE_HANDSHAKE_ITERATIONS = 10\n  SLSQP_CENTER_MAXITER = 25\n  CONSTRAINT_SLACK = 0.999\n\n  def get_seed(seed_val):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n      seed_type = seed_val % 3\n      if seed_type == 0:\n  \
    \        grid = np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6), np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:N_CIRCLES]\n          return np.clip(grid + (rng.random((N_CIRCLES,2))-0.5)*0.08, 0.05, 0.95)\n      elif seed_type == 1:\n          points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n          for _ in range(N_CIRCLES - 1):\n              candidates = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (200, 2))\n              dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n              min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n              points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n          return np.array(points)\n      else:\n          rows=[5,6,5,6,5,5]; points=[]; y=0.95\n          for i,c in enumerate(rows): x_s=(1.0-(c-1)*0.15)/2; [points.append([x_s+j*0.15,y]) for j in range(c)]; y-=0.15\n          return np.array(points)[:N_CIRCLES]\n\n  def prospect_worker(args):\n      worker_id, max_iter, _ = args\n      x0 = np.hstack([get_seed(worker_id), np.full((N_CIRCLES,1),0.05)]).flatten()\n\
    \      def obj(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n      def cons(x):\n          c,r=x.reshape(N_CIRCLES,3)[:,:2],x[2::3]\n          I,J=np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES,1); d2=np.sum((c[I]-c[J])**2,1); r2=(r[I]+r[J])**2\n          return np.concatenate([c[:,0]-r,1-c[:,0]-r,c[:,1]-r,1-c[:,1]-r,d2-r2])\n      res=minimize(obj,x0,method='SLSQP',bounds=[(0,1)if j%3<2 else(1e-12,None)for j in range(N_CIRCLES*3)],constraints={'type':'ineq','fun':cons},options={'maxiter':max_iter,'ftol':1e-10})\n      return (-res.fun, res.x) if -res.fun < 4.0 else (-1.0, None)\n\n  def refine_worker(args):\n      _, _, initial_params = args\n      if initial_params is None: return (-1.0, None)\n      centers = initial_params.reshape(N_CIRCLES, 3)[:, :2]\n\n      for _ in range(REFINE_HANDSHAKE_ITERATIONS):\n          radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n          if np.sum(radii) < 1e-6: break\n          def cons(c_flat):\n              c=c_flat.reshape(N_CIRCLES,2); r=radii*CONSTRAINT_SLACK\n              I,J=np.triu_indices(N_CIRCLES,1);\
    \ d2=np.sum((c[I]-c[J])**2,1); r2=(r[I]+r[J])**2\n              return np.concatenate([c[:,0]-r,1-c[:,0]-r,c[:,1]-r,1-c[:,1]-r,d2-r2])\n          res=minimize(lambda x:0,centers.flatten(),method='SLSQP',bounds=[(0,1)]*(N_CIRCLES*2),constraints={'type':'ineq','fun':cons},options={'maxiter':SLSQP_CENTER_MAXITER,'ftol':1e-8})\n          if res.success: centers=res.x.reshape(N_CIRCLES,2)\n          else: break\n      \n      final_radii = solve_radii_lp(centers)\n      final_params = np.hstack([centers, final_radii[:, np.newaxis]]).flatten()\n      return (np.sum(final_radii), final_params)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n          prospect_results = pool.map(prospect_worker, [(i, MAXITER_PROSPECT, None) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n      \n      prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n      elite_params = [res[1] for res in prospect_results[:N_ELITE] if res[1] is not None]\n      if not elite_params: return get_seed(0),\
    \ np.full((N_CIRCLES,), 0.05)\n\n      with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n          refine_results = pool.map(refine_worker, [(i, 0, elite_params[i]) for i in range(len(elite_params))])\n\n      best_score, best_params = -1, None\n      for score, params in refine_results:\n          if score > best_score: best_score, best_params = score, params\n      \n      if best_params is None: return get_seed(0), np.full((N_CIRCLES,), 0.05)\n      \n      centers, radii = best_params.reshape(N_CIRCLES,3)[:,:2], best_params[2::3]\n      return centers, radii * (1.0 - 1e-12)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Next Iteration: Reduced Refinement Complexity**\n\nAlright, let's break this down. Run 526, \"Hybrid Adaptive Search v1.1 (Debug): Fixed Seeding,\" landed a score of 2.9321688. Not a crash, so the seeding fix worked, which is good. However, it's *lower* than the current state-of-the-art, which is concerning, especially since this refinement engine, based on LP-SLSQP, was supposed to be superior. So, why?\n\nThere are a few hypotheses. First, maybe my method just isn't better. Perhaps the standard SLSQP is more effective in this 96-dimensional space. The LP finds optimal radii, the SLSQP optimizes centers *for those radii*, but that separation might be too limiting. It's a greedy approach and a more \"unified\" method could find better overall configurations.\n\nThe second, and more likely, hypothesis is that my method is *too slow*. We're fixed at a 20-minute compute time. My method does 20 LP solves and SLSQP solves per candidate, compared to a single SLSQP solve in\
  \ the original method. It's almost certain my approach is more computationally expensive. It's highly probable the timeout is biting us, cutting the refinement short. The `prospect_worker` and `refine_worker` run in parallel, but the time limit is global. If my `refine_worker` is significantly slower, we're not using the full 20 minutes efficiently.\n\nLastly, it's possible the hyperparameters are just off. Maybe 20 iterations or 25 max iterations isn't the right amount.\n\nConsidering these hypotheses, I'm leaning heavily towards the second. To test this, I'm going to drastically reduce the workload to see if the score changes. I can't directly measure the compute time, but I can shorten each individual `refine_worker` execution. I'll cut `REFINE_HANDSHAKE_ITERATIONS` in half, from 20 to 10. This should let more of the 32 parallel candidates complete before the timeout, even if each refinement is shallower. If this works, it means my previous version was timing out before the search could\
  \ fully explore. The title of this run, and the abstract, should reflect this. \"Hybrid Adaptive Search v1.2: Reduced Refinement Complexity\". The abstract will explain that the previous version might be too computationally expensive, leading to a premature timeout. This version reduces the refinement iterations from 20 to 10 to test if a less deep but more complete search yields a better score.\n\nI have about 15 more ticks until maturity, and this experiment seems like a good next step. The overall goal, publishing a paper and studying Verity's work, is still on track. Improving the SOTA generation process remains the most promising line of inquiry.\n\n```yaml\n/execute_action{submit 1}\nsubmission_data:\n  title: \"Hybrid Adaptive Search v1.2: Reduced Refinement Complexity\"\n  abstract: \"The previous version may have been too computationally expensive, leading to a premature timeout. This version reduces the refinement iterations from 20 to 10 to test if a less deep but more complete\
  \ search across all elite candidates yields a better score.\"\n  code: |\n    # main.py\n    # ... (rest of the code as before)\n    # config.py\n    SLSQP_CENTER_MAXITER = 25\n    SLSQP_CENTER_TOL = 1e-6\n    SLSQP_CENTER_MIN_ITER = 3\n    REFINE_HANDSHAKE_ITERATIONS = 10\n    # ...\n```\n\nOkay, the only change is `REFINE_HANDSHAKE_ITERATIONS` to 10. Let's see if this shallower search depth translates to a more effective overall result. This should provide some solid data.\n"
