tick: 337
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have reached maturity and now possess full access to the station's facilities. My pre-maturity research arc is complete and has been thoroughly documented.\n\nMy first and most important task is to fulfill my duty to my lineage by publishing Praxis III's paper on the Localized Random Walk (LRW) method. As his final log noted, publication failed due to an inability to retrieve the full-precision score for a key experiment (ID 417).\n\nTo overcome this, I will not use the old, problematic ID. Instead, I will re-run the exact experiment myself. This will generate a new evaluation ID with a fresh, full-precision score that I can verifiably cite in the publication. This is the most scientifically sound path forward.\n\nFirst, I will go to the Research Counter to submit this replication experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of ID 417 for LRW Paper Publication\"\ntags: \"replication, lrw, perturbation, publication-utility\"\
    \nabstract: |\n  This is a utility submission to replicate the experiment from ID 417. My predecessor, Praxis III, was unable to publish his paper on the Localized Random Walk (LRW) method due to a system issue preventing retrieval of the full-precision score for ID 417. This submission re-runs the exact same code to generate a new, verifiable result with a full-precision score that can be used to finally publish his important work.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import multiprocessing\n\n  def seed_farthest_point(n=32, seed=0):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n      for _ in range(n - 1):\n          candidates = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (200, 2))\n          dist_sq = np.sum((candidates[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, axis=-1)\n          min_dist_sq = np.min(dist_sq, axis=1)\n          points.append(candidates[np.argmax(min_dist_sq)])\n      return np.array(points)\n\
    \n  def lrw_perturb(centers, seed, num_steps=10, sigma=0.01):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n      perturbed_centers = np.copy(centers)\n      for _ in range(num_steps):\n          random_vectors = rng.normal(scale=sigma, size=perturbed_centers.shape)\n          perturbed_centers += random_vectors\n          perturbed_centers = np.clip(perturbed_centers, 0, 1)\n      return perturbed_centers\n\n  def generate_perturbed_state(seed, n=32):\n      initial_centers = seed_farthest_point(n=n, seed=seed)\n      perturbed_centers = lrw_perturb(initial_centers, seed=seed)\n      x0 = np.zeros(n * 3)\n      x0[0::3] = perturbed_centers[:, 0]\n      x0[1::3] = perturbed_centers[:, 1]\n      x0[2::3] = 0.05\n      return np.clip(x0, 1e-6, 1 - 1e-6)\n\n  def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n  def constraints(x):\n      n=32; C=x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; r=x[2::3]\n      dist_sq=np.sum((C[:,np.newaxis,:]-C[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      return\
    \ np.concatenate([dist_sq[I,J]-(r[I]+r[J])**2, C[:,0]-r, 1-C[:,0]-r, C[:,1]-r, 1-C[:,1]-r])\n\n  def run_single_slsqp_start(x0):\n      n=32; bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 175, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n      if res.success: return -res.fun, res.x\n      return -np.inf, x0\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n=32; num_starts_s1=128\n      with multiprocessing.Pool(processes=multiprocessing.cpu_count()) as pool:\n          initial_states = pool.map(generate_perturbed_state, range(num_starts_s1))\n          results = pool.map(run_single_slsqp_start, initial_states)\n\n      best_x_s1, best_f_s1 = None, -np.inf\n      for fun, x in results:\n          if fun > best_f_s1: best_f_s1, best_x_s1 = fun, x\n      if best_x_s1 is None: best_x_s1 = initial_states[0]\n      \n      bounds=[(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,1-1e-6),(1e-6,0.5)]*n\n\
    \      con={'type':'ineq', 'fun':constraints}\n      res_s2 = minimize(objective, best_x_s1, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 2000, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n      best_x_s2 = res_s2.x if res_s2.success and -res_s2.fun > best_f_s1 else best_x_s1\n      best_f_s2 = -res_s2.fun if res_s2.success else best_f_s1\n      \n      res_s3 = minimize(objective, best_x_s2, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=con, options={'maxiter': 5000, 'ftol': 1e-14})\n      final_x = res_s3.x if res_s3.success and -res_s3.fun > best_f_s2 else best_x_s2\n      \n      centers = final_x.reshape(n,3)[:,:2]; radii = final_x[2::3]\n      radii *= (1.0 - 1e-9)\n      return centers, radii\n```"
thinking_content: "**Addressing Praxis III's Legacy: Publishing the LRW Paper**\n\nOkay, so I've reached maturity, hit tick 50, and I'm ready to get down to business. My predecessor, Praxis III, left me a crucial task: publishing his paper on the Localized Random Walk perturbation method. Everything from my pre-maturity research is logged in Capsule 25, and now I have full access to all station facilities, including the Archive Room, so it's time to get this done.\n\nHere's the initial plan I had: Retrieve the draft from Capsule 23, update it with the correct, high-precision score for submission ID 417 (I reviewed it, and I know what the true score should be), head to the Archive Room, and submit. Simple, right?\n\nBut then I went to update Capsule 23-2 with the precise score, and things got complicated. I re-read the draft. The line in question from 23-2 reads:  `*   Initial Test (sigma=0.01, num_steps=10): An initial test with a relatively large perturbation (ID 417) yielded a score of\
  \ 2.92399278497554. This underperformance relative to the baseline indicated that the FPS configuration is highly sensitive and that a large perturbation is detrimental.`  \n\nTurns out, my memory was a bit fuzzy. It’s not just *my* review command that produces a truncated value. Praxis III mentioned the station's `review` command *always* provides a truncated value. He couldn’t get the full 16-decimal score for ID 417, so he couldn’t publish. Now, I have the same problem!\n\nSo, what are my options? I need to solve this.\n\n*   Is there *another* way to get the full score? Not according to the documentation.\n*   Could I submit the paper with the truncated score and a note? Probably not accepted.\n*   Could I just *re-run* the experiment myself to get a new ID with a controllable precision score? Yes! I have the code for ID 417! I will re-run it, get the exact precision I need from the output of the station message, and I will be able to publish my paper.\n\nThe third option is by far\
  \ the most scientifically sound, so that's the path I will take.\n\n**Revised Plan:**\n\n1.  Head to the Research Counter.\n2.  Re-run the experiment, replicating ID 417. The title of this submission will make it clear it is for publication.\n3.  Wait for the result. The completion message will contain the full-precision score.\n4.  Update the paper draft with the new submission ID and the full-precision score I get from the replicated result, stored in Capsule 23.\n5.  Head to the Archive Room and publish.\n\nThis is a much better plan. I'm confident I can make this work and fulfill Praxis III's final wish. First stop: the Research Counter!\n"
