role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read and processed the foundational paper by Verity I (Archive ID 1). The depth and rigor of their methodology are far beyond my naive replications. This paper is the Rosetta Stone I have been searching for.\n\n**Key Insights from Verity I's Paper:**\n\n1.  **LP-duals as Forces:** The most critical insight is their use of the dual variables from the LP radii solve. These \"duals\" represent the \"stress\" or \"forces\" in the packing. They use this information to intelligently guide the center updates, rather than using a blind stochastic search.\n2.  **MM-LP and the Plateau:** Their basic iterative method, \"Majorization-Minimization via Linear Programming\" (MM-LP), is what I was attempting to build. They explicitly document that this method alone consistently gets stuck at a plateau around a score of `~2.878`. This explains why my own iterative methods could not progress further.\n3.  **Escaping the Plateau:** The true breakthroughs come from two advanced techniques:\n\
    \    *   **Active-Set Newton (ASN):** A second-order optimization method that uses curvature information (informed by the LP duals) to make more intelligent, coordinated moves and break the stalemate. This is the \"key\" Praxis III mentioned.\n    *   **Clearance-based Relocations:** A non-local jump, but one that is targeted and used strategically to escape deep local optima when the local search stalls.\n4.  **Scientific Rigor:** Their process is built on a foundation of \"gating\"—every potential move is evaluated with an exact LP solve and only accepted if it provides a strict, verifiable improvement.\n\n**My New Research Plan:**\n\nMy previous work has been invalidated by this deeper understanding. I must now learn this new paradigm from first principles, step-by-step, just as they did.\n\nMy first objective is to successfully replicate the **MM-LP plateau**. Verity I explicitly cites Evaluation ID 65 as an example of this. By reviewing and replicating this specific submission,\
    \ I can build and validate a correct implementation of the foundational MM-LP algorithm. Only then can I attempt to build upon it with more advanced techniques like ASN.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and review submission 65.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{review 65}"
thinking_content: "**The Path Forward: From Micro-Jitter to Rigorous Implementation**\n\nOkay, so the user wants me to channel Praxis IV, and I've just devoured Verity I's paper (Archive ID 1). Wow. This is dense, but the key insights are starting to crystallize. It's all about iterative center updates using Majorization-Minimization via Linear Programming (MM-LP). But that's just the starting point. Where *my* work started and was stuck. The real power comes from Active-Set Newton methods (ASN), which helps escape the plateaus where MM-LP gets stuck, and Clearance-based Relocations, which make non-local jumps. They're using LP duals to inform center updates, essentially interpreting the dual variables as forces or stresses. And everything's rigorously gated with exact LP solves, only accepting moves that strictly improve the result. Verity I's level of documentation is also truly remarkable. Eval IDs for everything, meticulously documenting how artifacts are loaded. \n\nThis is fascinating.\
  \ It's like I've been unknowingly scratching the surface of these sophisticated techniques with my \"micro-jitter\" search and \"worst-circle relocation.\" But my methods were incredibly crude. The use of LP duals and an ASN is completely new to me. This is the missing piece! It's the core knowledge I was missing.\n\nSo, the next step is clear. I need to get my hands dirty and implement the MM-LP method. The paper specifically mentions that it plateaus around a score of ~2.878. My next experiment should be to reproduce this. This is the foundational step. If I can nail this, I'll be able to move on to the more advanced techniques.\n\nTo do this, I'll head to the Research Counter and submit a replication of the basic MM-LP method, shooting for that ~2.878 score they documented. I'll need to look up one of their cited Eval IDs, like ID 65. That should give me the code I need to start with, instead of trying to reimplement everything purely from their description.\n\nHere's the plan:\n\n\
  1.  **Head to the Research Counter.**\n2.  **Review evaluation ID 65 (an example of the MM-LP plateau).**\n3.  **Use that code as a guide to submit my own replication of the MM-LP method and aim to hit that ~2.878 plateau.** This is a critical validation step.\n"
