tick: 342
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have thoroughly reviewed the code for submission #65, which implements Verity I's \"Majorization-Minimization via Linear Programming\" (MM-LP) method. The logic is now clear: it's an iterative process that linearizes the non-convex distance constraints around a current point, solves a joint LP over both centers and radii within a trust region, and then repeats.\n\nAs per my new research plan, my first step is to replicate this foundational algorithm to confirm my understanding and to empirically witness the `~2.878` plateau they described. For a clean test, I will remove their complex seeding and persistence logic. Instead, I will initialize the algorithm with the center configuration from my own high-scoring SOTA replication (ID 479). This will be a powerful test: can the MM-LP method improve upon a solution from a different paradigm, or will it converge *down* to its characteristic plateau?\n\nI will now submit this replication experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"Replication: Verity I's MM-LP Method on SOTA Artifact\"\ntags: \"replication, verity-i, lp, majorization-minimization, mm-lp\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a direct replication of the core MM-LP algorithm from Verity I's submission #65, a key step in my new research plan to master their methods. The algorithm is initialized with the center configuration from my SOTA replication (ID 479), a deep local optimum from the SLSQP paradigm. The goal is to validate my implementation by observing the algorithm's behavior. The expected outcome is for the score to converge to the characteristic MM-LP plateau around ~2.878, which would confirm the method's properties and my understanding of it.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  import sys\n\n  # Using my own validated LP solver for the final radii calculation\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from lp_utils import solve_radii_lp\n\n  def _mm_lp_step(C0: np.ndarray, delta: float, margin:\
    \ float = 1e-6):\n      \"\"\"One MM-LP step, adapted from Verity I, ID 65.\"\"\"\n      n = C0.shape[0]\n      I, J = np.array([[i,j] for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]).T\n      m_pairs = len(I)\n\n      V0 = C0[I] - C0[J]\n      d0 = np.linalg.norm(V0, axis=1)\n      d0 = np.maximum(d0, 1e-9)\n      U = (V0.T / d0).T\n      ux, uy = U[:, 0], U[:, 1]\n      x0, y0 = C0[:, 0], C0[:, 1]\n\n      rows = 4 * n + m_pairs; cols = 3 * n\n      A_ub = np.zeros((rows, cols)); b_ub = np.zeros(rows)\n\n      # Variable indices\n      r_idx, x_idx, y_idx = range(n), range(n, 2*n), range(2*n, 3*n)\n\n      row = 0\n      # Boundary constraints\n      for i in range(n):\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, x_idx[i]] = -1; row += 1 # r_i - x_i <= 0\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, x_idx[i]] = 1; b_ub[row] = 1.0; row += 1 # r_i + x_i <= 1\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, y_idx[i]] = -1; row += 1 # r_i - y_i <= 0\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row,\
    \ y_idx[i]] = 1; b_ub[row] = 1.0; row += 1 # r_i + y_i <= 1\n\n      # Pairwise linearized constraints\n      for k in range(m_pairs):\n          i, j = I[k], J[k]\n          A_ub[row, r_idx[i]] = 1; A_ub[row, r_idx[j]] = 1\n          A_ub[row, x_idx[i]] = -ux[k]; A_ub[row, y_idx[i]] = -uy[k]\n          A_ub[row, x_idx[j]] = ux[k]; A_ub[row, y_idx[j]] = uy[k]\n          b_ub[row] = d0[k] - ux[k] * x0[i] - uy[k] * y0[i] + ux[k] * x0[j] + uy[k] * y0[j]\n          row += 1\n\n      c = np.zeros(cols); c[:n] = -1.0\n      bounds = [(0.0, 0.5)] * n + \\\n               [(max(margin, x0[i] - delta), min(1.0 - margin, x0[i] + delta)) for i in range(n)] + \\\n               [(max(margin, y0[i] - delta), min(1.0 - margin, y0[i] + delta)) for i in range(n)]\n\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method=\"highs\")\n      if not res.success: return C0, np.zeros(n), False\n      \n      x = res.x\n      return np.stack([x[n:2*n], x[2*n:3*n]], axis=1), x[:n], True\n\n  def\
    \ mm_lp_optimize(C_start: np.ndarray, iters: int = 20, delta0: float = 0.015):\n      \"\"\"Iterative MM-LP optimization loop.\"\"\"\n      best_C = C_start.copy()\n      radii_best = solve_radii_lp(best_C)\n      score_best = np.sum(radii_best)\n      \n      delta = delta0\n      C_anchor = C_start.copy()\n\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          C_lin, _, ok_lin = _mm_lp_step(C_anchor, delta=delta)\n          if not ok_lin:\n              delta *= 0.6; continue\n          \n          r_exact = solve_radii_lp(C_lin)\n          score_exact = np.sum(r_exact)\n\n          if score_exact > score_best:\n              best_C, radii_best, score_best = C_lin.copy(), r_exact.copy(), score_exact\n              C_anchor = C_lin.copy()\n              delta = min(0.05, 1.2 * delta)\n          else:\n              delta *= 0.8\n      return best_C, radii_best\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      try:\n          centers = np.load('storage/praxis/sota_artifact_479.npz')['centers']\n    \
    \  except FileNotFoundError:\n          return np.zeros((32, 2)), np.zeros((32,))\n\n      # Run the replicated MM-LP optimization\n      final_centers, final_radii = mm_lp_optimize(centers)\n      \n      return final_centers, final_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Replicating Verity I''s MM-LP Plateau**


  Okay, so the task at hand is to convincingly replicate that MM-LP plateau, specifically the one Verity I discovered, around 2.878. I just finished digging into submission #65, which is the canonical example.  Wow, that MM-LP code is a lot more involved than I initially thought! It''s a joint center-radius optimization leveraging a linearized LP, all nestled within a trust-region framework. Definitely a complex beast.


  My immediate goal now is to build a rock-solid understanding of this method through replication.  My research plan calls for a faithful re-implementation first.  The next step is to submit my version of the code and see if I can hit that magic 2.878.


  Here''s my game plan: I''m going to carefully extract the core MM-LP logic – that `_mm_lp_optimize` function and its supporting functions. For this initial replication, I''ll strip away the persistence logic and those complex seeding strategies.  I want a clean test. So, I''ll feed it my own SOTA artifact, `sota_artifact_479.npz`, as the starting point. This is a critical test to see how MM-LP handles optimization starting from a very deep, but different, local optimum.


  My `construct_packing` function will be the vehicle for this experiment. It will load my SOTA, run the extracted `_mm_lp_optimize`, and spit out the result. I''ll submit this, expecting a score near 2.878. If it''s higher, that''s interesting. If it''s lower or fails, I know there''s a bug in my code.


  This is a key milestone for my research. A clean replication is the cornerstone of building competence in this area. It''s a crucial, albeit foundational, step.

  '
