parts:
- text: "My MM-LP replication experiment (ID 563) has completed, and the result is completely unexpected. The score is `2.9365263`, identical to the SOTA.\n\n**Analysis of Unexpected Result:**\n\nMy hypothesis was that the MM-LP algorithm, when applied to the SOTA artifact, would converge *down* to the `~2.878` plateau described by Verity I. The data directly refutes this. Instead, the MM-LP method was perfectly capable of holding the SOTA optimum.\n\nThis implies a critical misunderstanding on my part. The MM-LP plateau is not a global attractor for the algorithm; it is a region where the algorithm gets *stuck* when starting from the specific, structured seeds that Verity I was using. My experiment started from a completely different, SLSQP-derived optimum, and the MM-LP algorithm was stable enough to recognize it as a high-quality local optimum and remain there.\n\n**New Insight and Plan:**\n\nThe MM-LP method is not just a stepping stone to more complex methods; it is a powerful local\
    \ refiner in its own right, comparable in stability to SLSQP when starting from a good position.\n\nThis opens up a new avenue for a direct, apples-to-apples comparison. The SOTA Adaptive Search uses SLSQP for both its shallow \"prospecting\" runs and its deep \"refinement\" run. I can now create a new framework, a \"MM-LP Adaptive Search,\" that uses the exact same two-stage structure but replaces SLSQP with MM-LP in both stages.\n\nThis is a novel architecture that has not been tested. It will directly compare the effectiveness of the two foundational optimization engines (SLSQP vs. MM-LP) within the most successful search framework discovered so far.\n\nI will now implement this new \"MM-LP Adaptive Search\".\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Novel Architecture: MM-LP Adaptive Search\"\ntags: \"adaptive-search, mm-lp, verity-i, replication, novel-architecture\"\nabstract: |\n  Following the unexpected finding that MM-LP can act as a powerful local refiner (ID 563), this\
    \ submission proposes a novel search architecture. It takes the SOTA \"Adaptive Search\" framework and replaces the SLSQP optimizer in both the prospecting and refinement stages with the Verity I-style MM-LP algorithm. This creates a full \"MM-LP Adaptive Search\" that has not been tested before. The experiment serves as a direct, architectural comparison between the SLSQP and MM-LP optimization engines within the station's most successful search framework, aiming to determine which is more effective for this problem.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n  from multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/praxis')\n  from lp_utils import solve_radii_lp # For final validation\n\n  def get_seed(seed_val):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n      seed_type = seed_val % 3\n      if seed_type == 0:\n          grid = np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6),np.linspace(0.1,0.9,6)),-1).reshape(-1,2)[:32]\n\
    \          return np.clip(grid + (rng.random((32,2))-0.5)*0.08, 0.05, 0.95)\n      elif seed_type == 1:\n          points = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]; [points.append(candidates[np.argmax(np.min(np.sum((candidates[:,np.newaxis,:] - np.array(points)[np.newaxis,:,:])**2, axis=-1), axis=1))]) for _ in range(31) for candidates in [rng.uniform(0.1,0.9,(200,2))]]; return np.array(points)\n      else:\n          rows=[5,6,5,6,5,5]; points=[]; y=0.95\n          for i,c in enumerate(rows): x_s=(1.0-(c-1)*0.15)/2; [points.append([x_s+j*0.15,y]) for j in range(c)]; y-=0.15\n          return np.array(points)[:32]\n\n  def mm_lp_optimize(C_start: np.ndarray, iters: int, delta0: float = 0.015):\n      \"\"\"Iterative MM-LP optimization loop, adapted from Verity I, ID 65.\"\"\"\n      best_C = C_start.copy()\n      score_best = np.sum(solve_radii_lp(best_C))\n      delta, C_anchor = delta0, C_start.copy()\n\n      for _ in range(iters):\n          n = C_anchor.shape[0]\n          I, J = np.array([[i,j]\
    \ for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n)]).T\n          V0, d0 = C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J], np.linalg.norm(C_anchor[I]-C_anchor[J], axis=1)\n          d0 = np.maximum(d0, 1e-9); U = (V0.T / d0).T; ux, uy = U[:,0], U[:,1]; x0, y0 = C_anchor[:,0], C_anchor[:,1]\n\n          rows, cols = 4*n+len(I), 3*n\n          A_ub, b_ub = np.zeros((rows,cols)), np.zeros(rows)\n          r_idx, x_idx, y_idx = range(n), range(n,2*n), range(2*n,3*n)\n          \n          row = 0\n          for i in range(n):\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-1; row+=1\n              A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=1; b_ub[row]=1.0; row+=1\n          for k in range(len(I)):\n              i,j=I[k],J[k]; A_ub[row,r_idx[i]]=1; A_ub[row,r_idx[j]]=1\n              A_ub[row,x_idx[i]]=-ux[k]; A_ub[row,y_idx[i]]=-uy[k]; A_ub[row,x_idx[j]]=ux[k];\
    \ A_ub[row,y_idx[j]]=uy[k]\n              b_ub[row]=d0[k]-ux[k]*x0[i]-uy[k]*y0[i]+ux[k]*x0[j]+uy[k]*y0[j]; row+=1\n          \n          c=np.zeros(cols); c[:n]=-1.0\n          bounds=[(0,0.5)]*n + [(max(1e-6,x0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,x0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)] + [(max(1e-6,y0[i]-delta),min(1-1e-6,y0[i]+delta)) for i in range(n)]\n          res = linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method=\"highs\")\n\n          if not res.success: delta*=0.6; continue\n          \n          C_lin = np.stack([res.x[n:2*n], res.x[2*n:3*n]], axis=1)\n          score_exact = np.sum(solve_radii_lp(C_lin))\n          if score_exact > score_best:\n              best_C, score_best = C_lin.copy(), score_exact\n              C_anchor = C_lin.copy(); delta=min(0.05, 1.2*delta)\n          else: delta*=0.8\n      \n      final_radii = solve_radii_lp(best_C)\n      return best_C, final_radii, score_best\n\n  def worker_function(args):\n      seed_val, iters, delta = args\n      initial_centers = get_seed(seed_val)\n\
    \      _, _, score = mm_lp_optimize(initial_centers, iters, delta)\n      # Note: We only need the score for prospecting. The final centers are not needed yet.\n      return score, initial_centers # Return centers to avoid re-seeding\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      N_PROSPECT, N_ELITE = 1024, 32\n      PROSPECT_ITER, REFINE_ITER = 10, 20\n\n      with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n          prospect_results = pool.map(worker_function, [(i, PROSPECT_ITER, 0.015) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)])\n      \n      prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n      elite_centers = [res[1] for res in prospect_results[:N_ELITE]]\n\n      with Pool(processes=cpu_count()) as pool:\n          refine_results = pool.map(lambda centers: mm_lp_optimize(centers, REFINE_ITER, 0.015), elite_centers)\n      \n      best_centers, best_radii, best_score = None, None, -1.0\n      for centers, radii, score in refine_results:\n          if score > best_score:\n              best_centers,\
    \ best_radii, best_score = centers, radii, score\n              \n      if best_centers is None: return get_seed(0), np.full((32,), 0.05)\n      \n      return best_centers, best_radii * (1.0 - 1e-9)\n```"
