- text: "**Phase 4: Risk Analysis and Contingency Planning**\n\nThis is the most complex algorithm I have attempted to implement. I must anticipate potential failure modes.\n\n1.  **Risk: Numerical Instability.** The Hessian `H` might be singular or poorly conditioned, especially if the active set is chosen poorly. My planned fix is to use Tikhonov regularization (`H + lambda*I`) to ensure the matrix is always invertible. If steps are still too large or erratic, I will need to tune `lambda` or the active-set threshold.\n\n2.  **Risk: High Computational Cost.** Assembling and solving a `64x64` linear system in each of the `asn_iters` for each of the `N_ELITE` candidates might be too slow and cause the submission to time out.\n    *   **Contingency 1:** I can reduce the number of `N_PROSPECT` candidates (e.g., to 768) to save time in the first stage, allocating more time for the more expensive refinement stage.\n    *   **Contingency 2:** I can reduce the number of `asn_iters` (e.g., from\
    \ 5 to 3). The ASN step is powerful, and even a few iterations might be sufficient to break the MM-LP stalemate.\n\n3.  **Risk: Stagnation (Failure to Improve).** The most significant risk is that even this more advanced method fails to improve the SOTA score. If my first `MM-LP+ASN` submission results in the same score, it would suggest the SOTA is in an incredibly deep and \"smooth\" local optimum that even a second-order method cannot escape.\n    *   **Contingency:** In this case, I would return to the second key idea from Verity I's paper: **\"clearance-based relocations.\"** I would design an experiment that uses my `MM-LP+ASN` refiner, but periodically relocates the single worst-performing circle to a new location. This would combine my most powerful local search with a powerful non-local jump, representing the complete replication of the Verity I philosophy.\n\n4.  **Risk: Token Budget.** My current token usage is `~60%`. Implementing and debugging this complex algorithm will\
    \ consume a significant amount of tokens. I must be efficient. I will develop the components in a single, well-structured utility file (`asn_utils.py`) to avoid code duplication. I will also make use of the Reflection Chamber again if I encounter conceptual roadblocks, as it is more token-efficient than repeated failed experiments.\n\nThis staged, verified, and risk-managed plan provides a robust path forward for tackling this complex but promising research direction.\n\n**Reflection Tick 5 / 5**\n\nNote: This is the last tick of your multi-tick reflection, meaning your next response should still be reflection and no station action will be parsed."
