tick: 357
role: model
parts:
- text: '**Phase 1: Deconstructing "Intelligent Relocation"**


    My previous attempt at relocation (ID 498, relocating the single worst circle) failed because it was too chaotic. It destroyed the global structure for a minor local gain. An *intelligent* relocation must preserve as much of the high-quality global structure as possible while making a targeted, non-local change designed to resolve a specific bottleneck.


    Verity I''s paper mentioned analyzing the "contact graph" and "short-cycle locks." This is the key. The SOTA solution is not just a collection of 32 points; it is a rigid geometric structure defined by a graph of circles that are touching or nearly touching. Simple, single-circle moves are ineffective because the rest of the graph "fights" the change.


    **The New Hypothesis: Coordinated Motif Relocation.** A more effective non-local move is to identify a small, semi-rigid group of circles—a "motif"—that forms a local bottleneck and move it as a single unit. Instead of moving one circle, I will move two or three.


    **The First Motif: The "Tightest Pair."** The simplest and most fundamental motif is a pair of circles that are in tight contact. This pair is often the anchor of a local region of high "stress" (high LP duals). My hypothesis is that **rotating this tightest pair around their mutual midpoint** is a structured, non-local move that can break geometric locks. This is not a random jiggle; it''s a coordinated rotation. By rotating the pair, I am exploring a different local arrangement while preserving their tight relationship, which is likely critical to the packing''s overall density. After the rotation, a full refinement of the entire configuration will be needed to see if a better local optimum has been discovered.


    This "Tightest Pair Rotation" is my first concrete implementation of an intelligent relocation strategy. It is non-local, structurally aware, and targeted at a specific bottleneck.


    **Reflection Tick 2 / 5**'
