role: model
parts:
- text: "The pruning operation was a success. My token usage has dropped from over 75% to a much healthier 41%. This gives me ample room to complete my paper and continue my research without immediate resource constraints.\n\nI am on a cooldown for further pruning actions, so my work in this room is complete. My next step is to resume the task that was interrupted: drafting my paper on the \"MM-LP Adaptive Search\" and the limits of local optimization.\n\nI will return to the Private Memory Room and add the next section to my paper draft, detailing the rigorous but ultimately unsuccessful attempts to surpass the SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{reply 26}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Paper Draft: Section 2 - Probing the SOTA: A Series of Null Results**\n\n  Upon establishing the new SOTA, a series of rigorous experiments were conducted to determine if further improvements were possible. These experiments, detailed below, consistently failed to surpass the SOTA score,\
    \ providing strong evidence of the stability and depth of this particular local optimum.\n\n  **1. Active-Set Newton (ASN) Refinement**\n\n  Our most significant effort was the implementation of a second-order optimization method, the Active-Set Newton (ASN) method, based on the foundational work of Verity I (Archive #1). This involved a staged, validated implementation of the entire ASN pipeline: identifying the active set via LP duals and slack (ID 578), assembling the Hessian and gradient (ID 581), and computing a stable, normalized Newton step (ID 588).\n\n  This powerful refinement engine, which uses second-order curvature information to guide its search, was then combined with our MM-LP optimizer into an \"MM-LP+ASN\" refiner. This engine was applied directly to the SOTA artifact in evaluation **ID 590**. The result was a score of **2.939572768266756**, identical to the SOTA. The second-order method, while mathematically more powerful, was unable to find an escape path from the\
    \ optimum.\n\n  **2. Non-Local Search via Motif Relocation**\n\n  Having exhausted local search methods, we pivoted to a non-local search paradigm based on \"Intelligent Motif Relocation.\" The hypothesis was that a structured, geometric transformation could \"jump\" the configuration to a better basin of attraction.\n\n  *   **Experiment 1 (ID 595):** We identified the \"tightest pair\" of circles in the SOTA configuration and applied a single, 90-degree rotation to them before re-optimizing. This proved too disruptive, resulting in a low score of `2.8893761`.\n\n  *   **Experiment 2 (ID 598):** We then tested a more subtle, parameterized approach, exploring a range of rotation angles (from 5 to 90 degrees) in parallel. This also failed to produce an improvement, returning the SOTA score of **2.9395727682715322**.\n\n  **Conclusion of Section 2**\n\n  The failure of both a powerful second-order local search (ASN) and a structured non-local search (Motif Rotation) to improve upon the\
    \ SOTA provides compelling evidence that this solution at `~2.93957` is an exceptionally robust local optimum. It is likely near-rigid, with a contact graph that resists both local and simple non-local perturbations. These null results are valuable, as they characterize the limits of this class of optimization techniques for the given problem.\n```"
