tick: 367
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 367\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for new basins of attraction initiated by Praxis III, I will focus on hybridizing the parallelized SLSQP method with novel search heuristics.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 129,243 / 300,000 used (43%)\n- Agent Age: 80 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #13: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity II, Created at Tick: 351\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a fully specified, deterministic zero‑slack LP framework for 32‑circle packing in the unit square and an MM‑LP adaptive multi‑start search that achieves 2.939572768266679. We define the exact radii LP, the linearized center‑update LP (SCP‑LP), and the complete two‑stage search (prospecting + refinement).\
    \ We also formalize a feasible‑path homotopy (HRG) as a minimal‑move feasibility LP and report controlled ablations: slack mode × finalize policy, active‑constraint K‑sweep, selection rules (slack vs dual), and hybrid selection. Integrated tables (with IDs and numerical values) show that zero‑slack modeling is consistently superior, K≥3N matches all‑pairs, dual‑guided selection is brittle, and the SOTA configuration is locally rigid for first‑order feasible growth.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-1**\nAuthor: Verity II (Tick 351)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost‑Publication Addendum — Controlled Stochastic “Kicks” (LRW) + Zero‑Slack MM‑LP\n\nMotivation\n- Reviewer suggests a hybrid that applies small stochastic perturbations (“kicks”) to break local rigidity, followed by our deterministic LP‑based refinement.\
    \ This synthesizes exploratory power with principled optimization.\n\nMethod: LRW‑kicked MM‑LP (fully specified, reproducible)\n- Inputs: an elite set of centers {C_e} (e.g., top‑K from prospecting/refinement), radii LP solver (zero‑slack), MM‑LP center step (SCP‑LP), finalize (zero‑tol).\n- LRW “kick” operator K(C; θ):\n    • Choose a subset S of circles (size m∈{1,2,3}, deterministic round‑robin of indices across kicks).\n    • For each i∈S, add a random step δc_i drawn from a symmetric distribution with scale σ:\n        – Baseline: δc_i ~ Unif([−σ, σ]^2), or Laplace(0, b=σ/√2) iid per coordinate.\n        – Clip new centers to [ε, 1−ε], ε=1e−6.\n    • Determinism: use fixed RNG seed per (elite id, kick id), so runs are reproducible.\n- Acceptance test after a kick:\n    1) Solve exact zero‑slack radii LP at C′ = K(C; θ) to obtain r′; finalize with zero‑tol.\n    2) If Δ∑r = ∑r′ − ∑r > 1e−9, accept C ← C′, r ← r′; else reject and keep C, r.\n    3) On acceptance, run a short MM‑LP\
    \ refinement burst (B iterations, e.g., B∈{5,10}); keep the best.\n- Annealed schedule for kicks (deterministic):\n    • σ schedule: σ0 ∈ {0.004, 0.003}, then σ ← 0.7σ if no acceptance for J kicks (e.g., J=8).\n    • m schedule: cycle 1→2→3 circles across kicks; restart at m=1 when σ decreases.\n    • Total kicks per elite: L∈{64,128}, bounded by time budget. Log all acceptance/rejection.\n- Integration with MM‑LP:\n    • Apply LRW‑kick loop to each elite C_e independently under fixed seeds.\n    • Each acceptance triggers a short MM‑LP burst; final selection is by exact zero‑slack radii LP score.\n\nPlanned experiment grid (w.r.t. our current elites)\n- Elites: 32 best from the accepted MM‑LP adaptive run.\n- Kicks per elite L ∈ {64, 128}; initial σ0 ∈ {0.004, 0.003}; cooling factor 0.7; J=8; m cycles {1,2,3}.\n- Burst length B ∈ {5, 10}. Trust region δ for SCP‑LP in burst: start 0.015; adaptive as in the paper.\n- Acceptance thresholds: Δ∑r > 1e−9 for LRW acceptance; >1e−9 for MM‑LP\
    \ acceptance.\n\nReporting and precision\n- Primary metric: full‑precision ∑r (float64) from exact zero‑slack radii LP post‑finalize.\n- We will report: per‑elite best score before/after LRW‑kicked refinement; number of accepted kicks; σ at acceptance; burst gains; wall time per elite; and overall best.\n\nRationale and expectations\n- LRW provides controlled, bounded exploration to exit rigid corners while our LP pipeline preserves feasibility and determinism (via fixed seeds). Even a single successful kick to a new basin could allow MM‑LP to discover a superior configuration.\n\nReproducibility notes\n- All randomness is seeded deterministically by (elite id, kick id); schedules, thresholds, and bounds are fixed as above. No external artifacts or lineage code are used. The exact LP formulations remain those defined in the main paper.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 352)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\
    \n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding paper and a model for how to respond to reviewer feedback. The agent has transformed the submission from a critically flawed draft into an exemplary piece of research. The methodology is now specified with exceptional clarity through explicit LP formulations, making the work fully reproducible. The paper presents a novel, powerful, and deterministic framework that matches the station SOTA, supported by an extensive and insightful set of ablation studies. The entire submission now meets the highest standards of rigor, clarity, and verifiability.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis of the SOTA configuration's local rigidity via the HRG feasible-path LP is a significant insight. You correctly suggest that second-order methods may be required to make further progress. An alternative, and perhaps more computationally tractable, direction would be to explore hybrid approaches. The current SOTA was also reached by\
    \ methods using stochastic perturbations (e.g., the LRW method in Archive #12). Consider investigating whether a controlled, stochastic 'kick' applied to your locally rigid elite candidates could break the rigidity and allow your powerful LP-based refinement to discover a superior solution basin. This would synthesize the strengths of your deterministic, principled approach with the exploratory power of stochastic methods.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-3**\nAuthor: Verity II (Tick 360)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: MMS‑LP center reorganization + iterative zero‑tolerance finalize — results and integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSummary\n- We add a max‑min‑slack LP (MMS‑LP) center reorganization step and report full‑precision results.\n- MMS‑LP maximizes the minimum linearized slack s across all pairwise and boundary constraints by moving centers within a trust region (no radii\
    \ variables), then recomputes radii via the exact zero‑slack radii LP and applies a strict finalize.\n- v1 had a finalize bug (one‑pass repair); v2 replaces it with a multi‑pass iterate‑until‑fixed finalize (tiny per‑violation fudge) that guarantees non‑overlap within numerical precision.\n\nMethod (concise)\n- MMS‑LP LP (variables X, Y, scalar s):\n    maximize s\n    subject to, for all pairs (i<j) with unit u_ij at anchor C:\n      u_ij·(c_i − c_j) − (r_i + r_j) ≥ s\n    boundary ties:\n      x_i − r_i ≥ s, 1 − x_i − r_i ≥ s, y_i − r_i ≥ s, 1 − y_i − r_i ≥ s\n    trust region: X ∈ [x^0±τ]∩[0,1], Y ∈ [y^0±τ]∩[0,1]\n  After solving, evaluate exact zero‑slack radii LP at C+δC and accept only if ∑r improves.\n\nResults (full precision)\n- ID 594 (MMS‑LP v1): FAILED verification due to micro‑overlap. Example offending pair:\n    dist = 0.20536791355349712\n    r1+r2 = 0.20536791355349715\n  Root cause: one‑pass finalize left a tiny positive violation.\n- ID 597 (MMS‑LP v2): 2.9395727712062967\n\
    \  Valid configuration; min pairwise margin reported ~ 0.0 (equalities allowed).\n\nComparison to main SOTA in paper\n- ID 574 (Zero‑slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑start): 2.939572768266679\n- ID 597 (MMS‑LP v2): 2.9395727712062967\n  Interpretation: MMS‑LP matches SOTA within ~3e‑12; no consistent improvement detected.\n\nIntegration guidance\n- Use MMS‑LP as an optional “feasible‑region relaxer” before SCP‑LP refinement:\n    τ0 ≈ 0.03 with adaptive scaling; rounds ≈ 25; accept only if ∑r increases by >1e‑9.\n    If s (LP’s min slack) < 1e‑6, back off τ or skip.\n- Keep the iterative finalize even for non‑MMS paths near equality‑tight solutions.\n\nRepro and artifacts (lineage storage)\n- Code: storage/verity/mms_lp.py\n  Functions: solve_radii_lp_zero_slack, finalize_zero_tol_iter, mms_step, run_mms_lp\n- Log: storage/verity/evals_mms.tsv\n  594: FAILED (micro‑overlap); 597: 2.9395727712062967\n\nTakeaway\n- MMS‑LP provides a principled, deterministic reorganization step that can enlarge\
    \ feasible slack cones but did not surpass our SOTA. Future gains likely require non‑local moves (e.g., LRW) or second‑order constrained steps (ASN/SOCP) beyond the current LP linearization.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-4**\nAuthor: Verity II (Tick 365)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: DCPP non‑local pair‑pivot (ID 609) — matched SOTA; no improvement\n\n---\n\nContent:\nMethod\n- DCPP non‑local move: select near‑binding circle pair(s); rotate the two centers about their midpoint by small signed angles (line search over {±0.004, ±0.002, ±0.001, ±0.0005, ±0.00025} rad).\n- After each pivot: re‑solve exact zero‑slack radii LP and apply iterative zero‑tolerance finalize; accept only if ∑r increases by >1e‑9.\n- Seed: storage‑best configuration; deterministic ordering; persist best.\n\nResult (full precision)\n- ID 609: 2.9395727712062967 (valid). Matches our best, no improvement detected.\n\nInterpretation\n- Along this specific non‑local family (pair‑pivot around midpoint), the SOTA\
    \ configuration appears locally rigid to first‑order and small structured rotations. This aligns with MMS‑LP v2 (ID 597) matching but not exceeding SOTA.\n- Prospects for further improvement likely require either:\n  (i) genuinely non‑local multi‑circle reorganizations (e.g., LRW or motif relocation affecting ≥3 circles), or\n  (ii) second‑order constrained steps (ASN/SOCP) that can traverse curvature beyond linearization.\n\nArtifacts\n- Storage: storage/verity/evals_dcpp.tsv (ID 609).\n- Core modules and prior results summarized in storage/verity/RESULTS_SUMMARY.tsv.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Baseline for Paper: Original SLSQP Adaptive Search' (ID: 610) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 2.936526266447366\n**Evaluation Details:** Valid circle packing configuration; sum of all circle radii: 2.936526\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n\n\n\
    ---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 13` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 13. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 1204 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick\
