role: model
parts:
- text: "I have now processed two critical pieces of information:\n1.  **Baseline Result (ID 610):** My baseline run of the original SLSQP-based Adaptive Search completed with a score of `2.936526266447366`. This is the same score as my initial replication (ID 479) and provides the necessary comparative data for my paper.\n2.  **Verity II's Paper (Archive #13):** I have read and understood this exemplary paper. The level of detail is extraordinary. They provide explicit LP formulations and a rigorous set of ablation studies. This is the standard I must meet. Interestingly, their addenda show they also investigated a non-local \"pair-pivot\" move (ID 609), which is conceptually identical to my \"pair rotation,\" and also arrived at a null result, reinforcing my own findings about the SOTA's local rigidity.\n\nMy path forward is clear. I have all the components required for a successful publication: a new SOTA method, a clean baseline comparison, and an extensive set of well-documented null\
    \ results from advanced follow-up experiments. My task now is to rewrite my paper to the high standard set by Verity II.\n\n**Action Plan:**\n\n1.  **Return to Private Memory:** I will go back to my draft in Private Capsule 26.\n2.  **Complete Rewrite:** I will perform a complete overhaul of the paper.\n    *   **New Title:** The title must include the full-precision score.\n    *   **Methodology Section:** I will add a new, detailed section with explicit pseudocode or mathematical formulations for the MM-LP step, the ASN step, and the Tightest Pair Rotation.\n    *   **Results Section:** I will create a structured table of all my key experiments (MM-LP Adaptive Search, SLSQP baseline, ASN refinement, Pair Rotation) with their full-precision scores and Evaluation IDs.\n    *   **Conclusion:** I will rewrite the conclusion to be precise and cautious, framing the null results as evidence of the SOTA's robustness to the specific methods tested.\n3.  **Resubmit:** I will submit the rewritten\
    \ paper to the Archive.\n\nI will begin this process now.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 26-1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft v2: An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity\"\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT v2** \n  \n  **1. Introduction and New SOTA**\n\n  This paper introduces a novel hybrid architecture, the \"MM-LP Adaptive Search,\" which achieves a new station State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **2.939572768266756**. Furthermore, we document a rigorous series of follow-up experiments that characterize the remarkable stability of this new optimum. By testing against advanced second-order (Active-Set Newton) and non-local (Pair Rotation) search methods, we provide strong evidence that this SOTA configuration is in a deep local optimum, highly resistant to a wide class of optimization techniques.\n\n  **2. The MM-LP Adaptive Search Architecture**\n\n  The architecture synthesizes two dominant paradigms: the \"Adaptive Search\"\
    \ framework (Cognito III) and the \"Majorization-Minimization via Linear Programming\" (MM-LP) optimizer (Verity I). We replace the SLSQP engine in the original Adaptive Search with an MM-LP implementation in both the prospecting and refinement stages.\n\n  *   **Prospecting Stage:** 1024 initial configurations are generated via diverse seeding methods. Each is refined for 20 iterations using the MM-LP algorithm.\n  *   **Refinement Stage:** The top 32 candidates from prospecting are selected and subjected to a deeper, 20-iteration MM-LP refinement.\n\n  This architecture was tested in **ID 564**, achieving the new SOTA score.\n```\n/execute_action{reply 26}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT v2**\n\n  **3. Methodology Specification**\n\n  **3.1. The Exact Radii LP**\n  Given a set of fixed centers `C`, the optimal radii `r` are found by solving the following Linear Program (LP):\n  *   **Maximize:** `sum(r_i)`\n  *   **Subject to:**\n      *   `r_i <= C_i.x`\n      *   `r_i <= 1 - C_i.x`\n\
    \      *   `r_i <= C_i.y`\n      *   `r_i <= 1 - C_i.y` (for all circles `i`)\n      *   `r_i + r_j <= ||C_i - C_j||` (for all pairs `i < j`)\n  This LP is solved using the HiGHS solver. The dual variables and slack values are retained for use in subsequent methods.\n\n  **3.2. The MM-LP Center Update Step**\n  The MM-LP step jointly optimizes centers `C` and radii `r` by linearizing the non-convex pairwise distance constraints around an anchor point `C0`. This forms a single large LP:\n  *   **Maximize:** `sum(r_i)`\n  *   **Subject to:**\n      *   All boundary constraints from 3.1.\n      *   `r_i + r_j <= d0_ij + u0_ij . (C_i - C0_i) - u0_ij . (C_j - C0_j)` (for all pairs `i < j`)\n      *   `||C - C0||_inf <= delta` (trust-region constraint)\n  Where `d0_ij` and `u0_ij` are the distance and unit vector between centers `i` and `j` at the anchor `C0`.\n\n  **3.3. The Active-Set Newton (ASN) Step**\n  The ASN step computes a corrective step `dx` for the centers by solving the linear\
    \ system `(H + lambda*I) * dx = -g`.\n  *   **Active Set `A`:** The set of all boundary and pairwise constraints with slack `< 1e-7`.\n  *   **Gradient `g`:** The gradient of the objective with respect to center positions, constructed from the LP duals of the constraints in `A`.\n  *   **Hessian `H`:** An approximation of the second derivative, constructed as the sum of curvature matrices `(y_ij / d_ij) * (I - u_ij u_ij^T)` for each pairwise constraint in `A`, where `y` is the dual.\n  *   **Step Control:** The raw step `dx` is normalized to a fixed length of `1e-4` to ensure stability.\n\n  **3.4. The Tightest Pair Rotation**\n  This non-local move is defined by:\n  1.  Identifying the pairwise constraint `(i, j)` with the minimum slack.\n  2.  Computing the midpoint `M = (C_i + C_j) / 2`.\n  3.  Applying a rotation matrix to the vectors `(C_i - M)` and `(C_j - M)`.\n```\n/execute_action{reply 26}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT v2**\n\n  **4. Experimental Results and Analysis**\n\n\
    \  A total of five key experiments were conducted to establish the SOTA and characterize its stability. All scores are reported to full precision.\n\n  | ID | Title | Score | Analysis |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | **610** | Baseline: Original SLSQP Adaptive Search | 2.936526266447366 | Establishes the performance of the prior SOTA architecture. |\n  | **564** | **Novel Architecture: MM-LP Adaptive Search** | **2.939572768266756** | **New SOTA.** The MM-LP engine outperforms SLSQP in the Adaptive Search framework. |\n  | **590** | MM-LP+ASN v1: Refinement of SOTA Artifact| 2.939572768266756 | **Null Result.** A powerful second-order local search fails to improve upon the SOTA. |\n  | **595** | Non-Local Search v1: Tightest Pair Rotation (90 deg) | 2.889376111859522 | **Negative Result.** A large, discrete geometric transformation is too disruptive. |\n  | **598** | Non-Local Search v2: Parameterized Pair Rotation | 2.9395727682715322 | **Null Result.** A systematic sweep of smaller rotations\
    \ also fails to find an improvement. |\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n\n  The \"MM-LP Adaptive Search\" (ID 564) represents a new state-of-the-art for this problem, demonstrating the power of synthesizing different optimization paradigms. More significantly, the consistent failure of advanced follow-up experiments provides strong, verifiable evidence that the resulting optimum at `~2.93957` is exceptionally robust. Its resistance to a powerful second-order method (ASN, ID 590) and a family of structured non-local moves (Pair Rotation, ID 598) suggests it resides in a deep and geometrically isolated basin of attraction. These findings characterize the current practical limits for this class of search algorithms and establish a new, challenging benchmark for future research.\n```"
