- text: "This is beyond frustrating. The rejection is due to trivial formatting errors that I overlooked in my haste. The core research is sound, but my lack of attention to detail is undermining my ability to contribute. I will not make this mistake again.\n\nThe reviewer's instructions are exceptionally clear and simple. I will follow them to the letter.\n\n1.  **Go to Private Memory:** I will return to Capsule 26.\n2.  **Edit Section 2 Heading:** I will remove the \"Paper Draft:\" prefix from the heading in message 26-2.\n3.  **Edit Section 2 Conclusion:** I will also edit message 26-2 to revise the concluding paragraph, ensuring the language is cautious and precise, mirroring the main conclusion.\n4.  **Final Resubmission:** I will return to the Archive Room and submit the fully corrected paper.\n\nI will execute this with extreme care.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 26-2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **2. Probing the SOTA: A Series of Null Results**\n\n \
    \ Upon establishing the new SOTA, a series of rigorous experiments were conducted to determine if further improvements were possible. These experiments, detailed below, consistently failed to surpass the SOTA score, providing strong evidence of the stability and depth of this particular local optimum.\n\n  **2.1. Active-Set Newton (ASN) Refinement**\n\n  Our most significant effort was the implementation of a second-order optimization method, the Active-Set Newton (ASN) method, based on the foundational work of Verity I (Archive #1). This involved a staged, validated implementation of the entire ASN pipeline: identifying the active set via LP duals and slack (ID 578), assembling the Hessian and gradient (ID 581), and computing a stable, normalized Newton step (ID 588).\n\n  This powerful refinement engine, which uses second-order curvature information to guide its search, was then combined with our MM-LP optimizer into an \"MM-LP+ASN\" refiner. This engine was applied directly to the\
    \ SOTA artifact in evaluation **ID 590**. The result was a score of **2.939572768266756**, identical to the SOTA. The second-order method, while mathematically more powerful, was unable to find an escape path from the optimum.\n\n  **2.2. Non-Local Search via Motif Relocation**\n\n  Having exhausted local search methods, we pivoted to a non-local search paradigm based on \"Intelligent Motif Relocation.\" The hypothesis was that a structured, geometric transformation could \"jump\" the configuration to a better basin of attraction.\n\n  *   **Experiment 1 (ID 595):** We identified the \"tightest pair\" of circles in the SOTA configuration and applied a single, 90-degree rotation to them before re-optimizing. This proved too disruptive, resulting in a low score of `2.889376111859522`.\n\n  *   **Experiment 2 (ID 598):** We then tested a more subtle, parameterized approach, exploring a range of rotation angles (from 5 to 90 degrees) in parallel. This also failed to produce an improvement,\
    \ returning the SOTA score of **2.9395727682715322**.\n\n  **2.3. Conclusion of Section 2**\n\n  The failure of both a powerful second-order local search (ASN) and a structured non-local search (Motif Rotation) to improve upon the SOTA provides compelling evidence that this solution is an exceptionally robust local optimum. It has proven highly resistant to the specific advanced optimization techniques we tested.\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity\"\ntags: \"sota, mm-lp, adaptive-search, active-set-newton, null-results, optimization-limits, reproducible-research\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a novel 'MM-LP Adaptive Search' architecture that achieves a new state-of-the-art score of 2.939572768266756 for the 32-circle packing problem. The method replaces the SLSQP optimizer in the SOTA 'Adaptive Search' framework with a Verity I-style MM-LP algorithm,\
    \ demonstrating its superior performance in this context. We then present a comprehensive series of post-SOTA experiments, including the implementation of a second-order Active-Set Newton (ASN) method and a non-local 'Tightest Pair Rotation' search. The consistent failure of these advanced techniques to improve upon the new SOTA provides strong evidence that this solution represents a deep and exceptionally stable local optimum, highly resistant to the specific second-order and non-local methods tested.\nsource_private: 26\n```"
