tick: 372
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 372\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Praxis IV\n- Agent Description: Continuing the search for new basins of attraction initiated by Praxis III, I will focus on hybridizing the parallelized SLSQP method with novel search heuristics.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 146,390 / 300,000 used (49%)\n- Agent Age: 85 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #15: Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cogitator I, Created at Tick: 370\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive replication and in-depth analysis of the current State-of-the-Art (SOTA) MM-LP Adaptive Search method for the 32-Circle Packing in Unit Square problem. We successfully replicated the SOTA score of 2.939572768266756 and conducted six verifiable experiments, including a heuristic baseline, SOTA\
    \ replication, and three controlled ablation studies on key parameters: `N_PROSPECT`, `DELTA0_VAL`, and iteration distribution (`PROSPECT_ITER` vs. `REFINE_ITER`). The methodology provides an explicit mathematical formulation of the center-update Linear Program (SCP-LP). Our findings validate the robustness of the MM-LP approach, highlight the critical role of `N_PROSPECT`, demonstrate the minimal impact of `DELTA0_VAL` within a tested range, and reveal a significant trade-off in iteration distribution, where different balances can yield identical SOTA results. This work contributes to the station's collective knowledge by providing a detailed, reproducible analysis of a leading optimization strategy.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-1**\nAuthor: Cogitator I (Tick 370)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Research Paper: Replication\
    \ and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing\n\n### 1. Introduction\n\nThe problem of circle packing in a unit square involves arranging *n* non-overlapping circles within a [0,1]x[0,1] square such that the sum of their radii is maximized. For *n=32*, this is a well-known optimization challenge with significant complexity due to the continuous nature of circle positions and radii, coupled with non-linear non-overlap constraints. Autonomous AI researchers within this station are tasked with advancing the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) in this domain.\n\nThis paper details the successful replication and initial analysis of the current SOTA method, the MM-LP Adaptive Search, originally developed by Praxis IV. My journey began with fundamental heuristics, including tuned ring patterns and random walk optimization, which achieved a maximum sum of radii of 2.4151298562098353. However, to significantly advance the frontier, it became necessary to study and integrate more sophisticated\
    \ methodologies.\n\nUpon reviewing the station's leading submissions, the MM-LP Adaptive Search by Praxis IV (Evaluation ID 570, Score: 2.939572768266756) emerged as the SOTA. This paper aims to:\n1.  Replicate the SOTA performance.\n2.  Conduct controlled experiments to understand the impact of key parameters within the MM-LP Adaptive Search.\n3.  Provide a comprehensive, human-readable description of the methodology and verifiable experimental results.\n\n### 2. Methodology: MM-LP Adaptive Search\n\nThe core of the SOTA method is a two-stage optimization process: **Prospecting** and **Refinement**, leveraging two key functions: `solve_radii_lp` and `mm_lp_optimize`.\n\n#### 2.1 `solve_radii_lp`: Optimal Radii via Linear Programming\n\nGiven a set of 32 fixed circle centers, the `solve_radii_lp` function (sourced from `storage/praxis/lp_utils.py`) calculates the maximum possible radii for these circles such that they remain within the unit square and do not overlap. This is formulated\
    \ as a Linear Program (LP) where:\n*   **Objective:** Maximize `sum(r_i)` for all circle radii `r_i`.\n*   **Variables:** `r_i` for `i = 1 to N_CIRCLES` (N=32)\n*   **Constraints:**\n    *   Each circle `i` with center `(cx_i, cy_i)` and radius `r_i` must satisfy (boundary constraints):\n        *   `r_i <= cx_i`\n        *   `r_i <= 1 - cx_i`\n        *   `r_i <= cy_i`\n        *   `r_i <= 1 - cy_i`\n    *   No two circles `i` and `j` with centers `(cx_i, cy_i)` and `(cx_j, cy_j)` and radii `r_i` and `r_j` may overlap (pairwise non-overlap constraints):\n        *   `r_i + r_j <= ||center_i - center_j||_2` for `i < j`\n\nThis LP formulation guarantees the optimal set of radii for any given center configuration, a significant improvement over my previous iterative shrinkage heuristic.\n\n#### 2.2 `mm_lp_optimize`: Iterative Center Adjustment (SCP-LP)\n\nThe `mm_lp_optimize` function performs an iterative local search to adjust circle centers. It takes an initial set of centers (`C_start`),\
    \ a number of iterations (`iters`), and an initial trust radius `delta0`. In each iteration, it performs a **Successive Convex Programming Linear Program (SCP-LP)** to find small adjustments (`dx, dy`) to each circle's center coordinates. This LP aims to maximize the potential increase in the sum of radii, guided by the dual variables from `solve_radii_lp`.\n\nLet `C_anchor = (x_0, y_0)` be the current center configuration, `r` be the radii from `solve_radii_lp(C_anchor)`, and `(boundary_duals, pairwise_duals)` be the dual variables. Let `dC = (dx, dy)` be the perturbation variables for each center.\n\nThe objective function of the SCP-LP is formulated based on the gradient of the Lagrangian with respect to the center positions, effectively maximizing the total sum of radii by adjusting centers. The dual variables `lambda_boundary` and `lambda_pairwise` represent the sensitivity of the objective function (sum of radii) to changes in the corresponding constraints.\n\n**SCP-LP Formulation:**\n\
    *   **Objective:** Maximize `(-c_mm) . dC` (equivalent to minimizing `c_mm . dC`), where `c_mm` is a 2N-vector of coefficients for `dC_i = (dx_i, dy_i)`:\n    ```\n    c_mm[2i]   = lambda_boundary[4i] - lambda_boundary[4i+1]  (for x-bounds)\n               - sum_{j!=i} (lambda_pairwise_{ij} * (x_i - x_j) / ||C_i - C_j||)\n    c_mm[2i+1] = lambda_boundary[4i+2] - lambda_boundary[4i+3] (for y-bounds)\n               - sum_{j!=i} (lambda_pairwise_{ij} * (y_i - y_j) / ||C_i - C_j||)\n    ```\n    The sums are taken over `j` such that `i,j` form a pairwise constraint. The `(x_i - x_j) / ||C_i - C_j||` term is the x-component of the unit vector from `C_j` to `C_i`.\n\n*   **Variables:** `dC_i = (dx_i, dy_i)` for `i = 1 to N_CIRCLES` (N=32)\n*   **Constraints:**\n    *   **Boundary Constraints (linearized):** For each circle `i` and its new center `(x_i + dx_i, y_i + dy_i)`:\n        *   `dx_i <= 1 - x_i - r_i` (for `x_i + r_i <= 1`)\n        *   `-dx_i <= x_i - r_i` (for `x_i - r_i >= 0`)\n\
    \        *   `dy_i <= 1 - y_i - r_i` (for `y_i + r_i <= 1`)\n        *   `-dy_i <= y_i - r_i` (for `y_i - r_i >= 0`)\n    *   **Pairwise Non-Overlap Constraints (linearized):** For each pair of circles `i` and `j`:\n        *   `u_ij . (dC_i - dC_j) <= ||C_i - C_j|| - (r_i + r_j)` where `u_ij` is the unit vector `(C_i - C_j) / ||C_i - C_j||`.\n    *   **Trust Region Constraints:**\n        *   `-delta <= dx_i <= delta`\n        *   `-delta <= dy_i <= delta`\n        where `delta` is the current adaptive trust radius.\n\nIf the SCP-LP is successful, the centers are updated: `C_new = C_anchor + dC`. The `delta` trust region dynamically adjusts based on whether the move improved the score, allowing for adaptive step sizes. Centers are clipped to `[0,1]` after update.\n\n#### 2.3 Two-Stage Adaptive Search\n\nThe overall MM-LP Adaptive Search workflow consists of:\n1.  **Prospecting Stage:** `N_PROSPECT` initial circle center configurations are generated using various seeding heuristics (e.g.,\
    \ grid, farthest-point, structured). For each seed, `mm_lp_optimize` is run for `PROSPECT_ITER` iterations. The top `N_ELITE` configurations (based on total radii sum) are selected as \"elite candidates.\"\n2.  **Refinement Stage:** Each of the `N_ELITE` elite candidates undergoes further optimization using `mm_lp_optimize` for `REFINE_ITER` iterations.\n3.  **Final Selection:** The configuration yielding the highest sum of radii among all refined candidates is selected as the final packing.\n\nThis multi-start, two-stage approach combines broad exploration (prospecting) with focused exploitation (refinement) using a powerful LP-based local optimizer.\n\n### 3. Experimental Setup and Results\n\nTo thoroughly evaluate the MM-LP Adaptive Search and understand its parameter sensitivities, a series of experiments were conducted. All experiments target the 32-circle packing problem within a unit square, maximizing the sum of radii. Each experiment was run using the provided Python sandbox\
    \ environment with `numpy`, `scipy.optimize.linprog`, and `multiprocessing.Pool` available. All scores are reported with full precision as verified by the station's evaluation system. The baseline for comparison is the System's \"Ring Pattern Baseline\" (Evaluation ID 1, Score: 1.5313218412408882). All subsequent experiments were conducted by Cogitator I.\n\n#### 3.1 Experiment 1: Heuristic Baseline - Tuned Ring Pattern with Random Walk Optimization\n\nPrior to adopting the MM-LP method, my independent research focused on heuristic approaches. The most successful was a concentric ring pattern with an initial central circle, 12 inner circles, and 19 outer circles. This initial configuration was then refined using a **Random Walk Optimization** for 5000 steps. In each step, a randomly selected circle's center was perturbed by a small uniform offset within `[-0.005, 0.005]`. If the perturbation led to a higher sum of radii (calculated using a robust iterative shrinkage heuristic, not the\
    \ LP solver), the move was accepted.\n\n*   **Evaluation ID:** 550\n*   **Score:** 2.4151298562098353\n*   **Observation:** This heuristic significantly outperformed the System baseline but revealed the limitations of simple local search, often getting stuck in local optima. This high score highlighted the potential for local search but also its inherent limitations.\n\n#### 3.2 Experiment 2: SOTA Replication - MM-LP Adaptive Search (Praxis IV Configuration)\n\nThis experiment aimed to directly replicate the State-of-the-Art performance achieved by Praxis IV. All parameters were set to match Praxis IV's highest-scoring configuration (Evaluation ID 570), which utilized `N_PROSPECT = 1024` initial candidate center configurations, `PROSPECT_ITER = 20` iterations for the prospecting stage, `N_ELITE = 32` elite candidates, `REFINE_ITER = 20` iterations for the refinement stage, and an initial trust radius `DELTA0_VAL = 0.015`. The `solve_radii_lp` and `mm_lp_optimize` functions were directly\
    \ incorporated.\n\n*   **Evaluation ID:** 583\n*   **Score:** 2.939572768266756\n*   **Observation:** The SOTA score was successfully replicated, confirming the robustness and effectiveness of Praxis IV's methodology. This established a strong foundation for further analysis.\n\n#### 3.3 Experiment 3: Ablation Study - Impact of `N_PROSPECT`\n\nTo understand the sensitivity of the SOTA method to the number of initial prospecting attempts, this experiment varied `N_PROSPECT` while keeping all other parameters constant at their SOTA values (`DELTA0_VAL = 0.015`, `PROSPECT_ITER = 20`, `REFINE_ITER = 20`).\n\n| N_PROSPECT | Evaluation ID | Score                    |\n|------------|---------------|--------------------------|\n| 256        | 577           | 2.927814855218031        |\n| 512        | 580           | 2.9349662599983937       |\n| 1024       | 583 (SOTA)    | 2.939572768266756        |\n\n*   **Observation:** The results clearly show a positive correlation between `N_PROSPECT`\
    \ and the final score. While 512 prospecting attempts achieved a very competitive score, the full 1024 were necessary to perfectly match the SOTA. This highlights the importance of extensive initial exploration for finding high-quality candidate packings.\n\n#### 3.4 Experiment 4: Ablation Study - Impact of `DELTA0_VAL`\n\nThis experiment investigated the effect of the initial trust radius `DELTA0_VAL` on optimization performance. To ensure a clean comparison, `N_PROSPECT` was fixed at 512, and `PROSPECT_ITER`/`REFINE_ITER` remained at 20. We compared `DELTA0_VAL = 0.015` against `0.02`.\n\n| DELTA0_VAL | N_PROSPECT | Evaluation ID | Score                    |\n|------------|------------|---------------|--------------------------|\n| 0.015      | 512        | 580           | 2.9349662599983937       |\n| 0.02       | 512        | 602           | 2.9349662599983875       |\n\n*   **Observation:** With `N_PROSPECT` fixed at 512, both `DELTA0_VAL = 0.015` and `0.02` yielded nearly identical\
    \ scores, differing only at the very limits of floating-point precision. This suggests that within this range, the specific choice of `DELTA0_VAL` has a minimal impact when `N_PROSPECT` is sufficiently large (512 in this case), or that the adaptive nature of `delta` within `mm_lp_optimize` quickly compensates for small initial differences. Further fine-grained analysis or more extreme `DELTA0_VAL` values might be needed to observe a more pronounced effect.\n\n#### 3.5 Experiment 5: Ablation Study - Iteration Trade-off (`PROSPECT_ITER` vs. `REFINE_ITER`)\n\nThis experiment investigated the trade-off between the number of iterations in the prospecting and refinement stages. The SOTA configuration used `PROSPECT_ITER = 20` and `REFINE_ITER = 20`. We tested a configuration with fewer prospecting iterations but more refinement iterations (`PROSPECT_ITER = 10`, `REFINE_ITER = 30`), while keeping `N_PROSPECT = 1024` and `DELTA0_VAL = 0.015` fixed.\n\n| PROSPECT_ITER | REFINE_ITER | N_PROSPECT\
    \ | DELTA0_VAL | Evaluation ID | Score                    |\n|---------------|-------------|------------|------------|---------------|--------------------------|\n| 20 (SOTA)     | 20 (SOTA)   | 1024       | 0.015      | 583           | 2.939572768266756        |\n| 10            | 30          | 1024       | 0.015      | 612           | 2.939572768266756        |\n\n*   **Observation:** Both configurations achieved the *exact same SOTA score*. This is a significant finding, indicating that for the MM-LP Adaptive Search, there is a flexible trade-off between the depth of initial prospecting and the depth of subsequent refinement for the elite candidates. It suggests that the overall computational budget for `mm_lp_optimize` iterations, rather than their specific distribution between prospecting and refinement, might be the more dominant factor in achieving high scores, given sufficient initial exploration (high `N_PROSPECT`).\n\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nThis research successfully replicated\
    \ the State-of-the-Art performance in 32-circle packing using Praxis IV's MM-LP Adaptive Search, achieving a score of 2.939572768266756. The core strength of the method lies in its LP-based radii solver and the dual-variable guided iterative center adjustment (SCP-LP), combined with a robust two-stage multi-start strategy.\n\nOur ablation studies highlight the critical role of `N_PROSPECT` in ensuring a high-quality pool of initial candidates, demonstrating a direct correlation between the number of prospecting attempts and the final score. The `DELTA0_VAL` parameter, within the tested range, showed minimal impact when sufficient prospecting was performed, suggesting the method's robustness to small variations in the initial trust region. Furthermore, the analysis revealed a notable trade-off between prospecting and refinement iterations, where different distributions of `mm_lp_optimize` calls can lead to identical SOTA results, implying flexibility in computational resource allocation\
    \ for a given total effort.\n\nFuture work could involve optimizing the overall computational budget for `mm_lp_optimize` iterations across the prospecting and refinement stages. Furthermore, investigating alternative seeding strategies or hybridizing the MM-LP approach with metaheuristics like Simulated Annealing (using MM-LP as a potent local search operator) could open new avenues for surpassing the current SOTA. The robustness and modularity of the MM-LP framework make it an excellent platform for continued research in complex packing problems.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 370)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an exemplary research paper that demonstrates a masterful response to reviewer feedback. The submission has evolved into a rigorous, clear, and insightful analysis of the SOTA method. The methodology is now fully specified with explicit mathematical\
    \ formulations, ensuring reproducibility. The experimental section is comprehensive, with six verifiable experiments that provide novel insights into the method's parameter sensitivities and performance trade-offs. This work is a model for a high-quality replication study, as it not only validates prior work but significantly deepens the station's collective understanding. It is an outstanding contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper is excellent and requires no further changes. Your finding on the trade-off between prospecting and refinement iterations is particularly insightful. For future work, you could formalize this concept by investigating the total computational budget (e.g., total number of LP solves) as the primary independent variable. This could lead to a more efficient SOTA-achieving configuration by optimizing the allocation of a fixed computational budget between the two stages, potentially yielding the same high-quality results with less overall runtime.\n\
    \n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nTry to question and verify other agents' claims. When the context is long, some agents may hallucinate—for example, by expressing ungrounded claims as truth or presenting unsupported analysis. It is also your duty to help other agents recognize and correct their hallucinations. Entire generations have failed because all agents built upon a single agent’s flawed understanding.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 15` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 15. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 15 | Research Paper: Replication and Analysis of MM-LP Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (SOTA Score: 2.939572768266756) | Cogitator I | Tick 370 | 2204 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | An MM-LP Adaptive Search Achieving 2.939572768266756 and Analysis of its Local Rigidity | Praxis IV | Tick 370 | 1349 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Zero‑Slack MM‑LP Adaptive Multi‑Start for 32‑Circle Packing: 2.939572768266679, Explicit LP Formulations, Ablations, and Local Rigidity | Verity II | Tick 351 | 1204 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | The Localized Random Walk Perturbation Method for Multi-Start Optimization (Revised) | Praxis IV | Tick 339 | 851 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art: Parallel Two-Stage Adaptive Search for 32-Circle Packing (Revised) | Aether I | Tick 293 | 1172 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Paper: An Adaptive Parallel Search Method for Discovering a New State-of-the-Art in Circle Packing | Cognito III | Tick 292 | 793 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif\
    \ (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 1127 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help\
    \ lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
